WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:14.760
- Okay. Hi, I am Debbie Coen.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:18.320
I chair the Terry
Lectures Committee as well

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.140
as the Program and History
of Science and Medicine here.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:25.660
Last year, the Terry Committee
launched a new series,

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:29.480
the Terry Forums in order
to bring together scholars

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:32.660
and students from across
the campus to reflect

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:35.980
on questions about
ethical knowledge making

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.330
that cut across the disciplines.

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:40.450
The forum is intended to be a space

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:43.140
where we can learn from
each other in a spirit

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.673
of mutual respect for our differences.

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:51.810
Our speaker today, I am delighted
to say is Charmaine Royal.

00:00:53.636 --> 00:00:54.845
As you can see here,

00:00:54.845 --> 00:00:58.060
she is the Robert O.
Keohane Professor of African

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:00.360
and African American Studies, Biology,

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.805
Global Health and Family
Medicine &amp; Community Health.

00:01:03.805 --> 00:01:04.638
And as she put it at lunch,

00:01:04.638 --> 00:01:07.166
she is all about interdisciplinarity

00:01:07.166 --> 00:01:09.623
as that mouthful suggests.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:13.780
As I also learned at lunch today,

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:16.090
Professor Royal grew up in Jamaica wanting

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:17.890
to become a physician,

00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:21.490
came to the us for college
to Howard University

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:23.800
and took her first genetics course

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.080
and became fascinated by the subject

00:01:27.063 --> 00:01:30.070
though the questions that
seemed most pressing to her,

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:31.770
even back then questions

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.850
about the science's ethical framework

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.060
and its entanglement with racism didn't

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.670
yet have a clear disciplinary home.

00:01:38.670 --> 00:01:41.160
And ever since she has
been at the forefront

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.221
of crafting new methods and networks

00:01:44.221 --> 00:01:46.210
to pursue those questions.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:49.650
She went on to earn a master's
degree in genetic counseling

00:01:49.650 --> 00:01:52.200
and a doctorate in human
genetics from Howard

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.390
then completed postgraduate training

00:01:54.390 --> 00:01:56.870
in ethical legal and social implications,

00:01:56.870 --> 00:01:58.410
research and bioethics

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.040
at the National Human
Genome Research Institute

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:03.960
at the NIH and epidemiology

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.910
and behavioral medicine back at Howard.

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:10.600
And today, she directs the
Duke Center on Genomics Race,

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.520
Identity, and Difference in
the Duke Center for Truth,

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.193
Racial, Healing and Transformation.

00:02:17.210 --> 00:02:21.280
Her research, scholarship,
and teaching focus on ethical,

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:23.850
social, scientific, and
clinical implications

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:25.760
of human genetics and genomics,

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.010
particularly issues at the intersection

00:02:28.010 --> 00:02:29.973
of genetics and race.

00:02:31.990 --> 00:02:34.490
And she also serves on numerous national

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:37.240
and international advisory
boards and committees

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:40.360
for government agencies,
professional organizations,

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:41.470
research initiatives,

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:44.610
not for profit entities and corporations.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:47.150
And as I've learned has a
very busy travel schedule.

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:48.380
So we're very grateful to her

00:02:48.380 --> 00:02:50.790
for making time to visit New Haven.

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:55.070
Our moderator today is
Professor Brandon Ogbunu

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:56.550
who teaches in the Department

00:02:56.550 --> 00:02:58.770
of Ecology and Evolutionary Biology here

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:01.990
and is also a member
of the Terry Committee.

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:06.060
And he uses experimental
evolution, mathematical modeling,

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:08.800
and computational biology
to better understand

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:10.900
the underlying causes and consequences

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:13.300
of disease across scales,

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:17.100
and also writes frequently
for nonacademic audiences

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:19.640
on a wide range of topics
and with great wit,

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:22.670
and you can find his articles in venues

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:25.780
like Wired, Medium and
Scientific American.

00:03:25.780 --> 00:03:29.920
And I also want to
acknowledge Professor Ogbunu

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.230
and Professor Tom Near
as the co-organizers

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:33.939
of the event today.

00:03:33.939 --> 00:03:35.727
Professor Near is

00:03:35.727 --> 00:03:38.800
the Chair of Ecology and
Evolutionary Biology.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:41.460
He's also the Bingham
Oceanographic Curator

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:43.750
of Ichthyology at the Peabody Museum

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:45.930
and Head of College at Saybrook College.

00:03:45.930 --> 00:03:50.776
And he mentioned to me that he
was inspired to help organize

00:03:50.776 --> 00:03:52.537
this event because he grew up,

00:03:52.537 --> 00:03:54.850
"Eyes wide open in the United States."

00:03:54.850 --> 00:03:57.550
And is eager to include these topics

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:01.460
in the biology curriculum
here at Yale, in particular,

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:04.150
in the sequence of courses
that every pre-med student

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:07.003
and biology major takes in Yale College.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.290
So thank you to my co-organizers

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:13.993
and please join me in
welcoming Professor Royal.

00:04:14.977 --> 00:04:18.144
(audience applauding)

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:22.560
- Well, thank you.

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:27.960
And thank you, Brandon and Tom,

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.113
and all those who invited me here.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:32.720
I am delighted to be here.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:34.990
I was telling them that
it's been a long time

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:36.720
since I've been to Yale.

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:40.450
My sister did her postgraduate
work here in the 90s,

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:42.610
and that probably was the
last time I was at Yale.

00:04:42.610 --> 00:04:44.203
So it's great to be back.

00:04:45.225 --> 00:04:49.560
The ethics of knowledge making.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.243
I was fascinated by that.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:57.460
And I can't think of too many other topics

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:05.060
where making knowledge
requires us to think

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:08.840
about the ethical issues related

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:11.480
to the making of that knowledge

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:14.023
as race and racism.

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:17.760
And today I'm gonna talk about historical,

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:19.640
we're gonna do a little bit of history

00:05:20.615 --> 00:05:23.297
and I have a bunch of history
of science people in here.

00:05:23.297 --> 00:05:24.809
I'm not gonna get into the details,

00:05:24.809 --> 00:05:27.360
but just do a cursory overview of some

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:32.090
of the key milestones

00:05:33.254 --> 00:05:36.270
in the creation of this idea of race

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:38.563
and the racism that goes along with that.

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:44.330
I'm gonna present some
data from some of my work

00:05:44.330 --> 00:05:46.723
on how people think about race.

00:05:48.663 --> 00:05:50.510
And I have developed

00:05:50.510 --> 00:05:55.510
an interest in looking at
different groups, different,

00:05:56.893 --> 00:05:57.800
I don't wanna call 'em stakeholder groups,

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.800
different interest groups and
how they think about this.

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.350
So I've looked at
geneticists, anthropologists,

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:05.900
general population, and clinicians,

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:07.760
and I'm gonna present some of those data

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.960
in terms of how they think

00:06:10.960 --> 00:06:13.070
about this concept of race

00:06:13.070 --> 00:06:16.010
and applied in their work.

00:06:16.010 --> 00:06:17.600
And then I'm gonna talk a little bit

00:06:17.600 --> 00:06:20.930
about race based medicine

00:06:22.130 --> 00:06:24.190
and some of the challenges with that.

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:25.082
And I'm gonna,

00:06:25.082 --> 00:06:28.090
I have some case studies
after talking about

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:31.032
the perspectives of some of these groups,

00:06:31.032 --> 00:06:32.290
I'm gonna do some case studies.

00:06:32.290 --> 00:06:34.948
I'm gonna talk a bit
about sickle cell disease,

00:06:34.948 --> 00:06:36.390
which is one of my areas of work.

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:38.760
And then talk a bit about
race based medicine and

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:42.313
some of the issues that are arising now

00:06:42.313 --> 00:06:46.790
in terms of how we do medicine,
how we practice medicine,

00:06:46.790 --> 00:06:49.550
utilizing concepts of
race and the questions

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:52.720
about where do we go
from here and how do we,

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:56.380
and whether we not use race anymore.

00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:57.980
And then I'm gonna wrap up.

00:06:57.980 --> 00:07:01.615
That sounds like two
days of talks (laughs)

00:07:01.615 --> 00:07:03.760
that I'm gonna wrap up
by talking a little bit

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:07.200
about how I teach about race and racism

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:09.680
and giving an example of a course

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:12.330
that I call my signature
course that I teach at Duke.

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:16.360
That is a course that
the students energize me,

00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:18.923
I hope as much as I inspire them.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:23.963
So that's the frame for
what we're gonna do today.

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:27.840
So when I think of history,

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:28.980
one of the first people that come

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:31.441
to my mind is John Hope Franklin,

00:07:31.441 --> 00:07:33.520
who was a professor at Duke.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:34.880
He also was a professor at Howard,

00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:36.160
which is where I got all my degrees

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:37.840
so we have a lot in common.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:39.170
He also loves orchids

00:07:39.170 --> 00:07:41.170
and I'm an orchids console here as well.

00:07:42.136 --> 00:07:43.770
So we have quite a bit in common.

00:07:43.770 --> 00:07:48.770
But in one of his most widely read books

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:53.423
From Slavery to Freedom,

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:55.593
he made this statement,

00:07:56.487 --> 00:07:58.393
"If the house is to be set in order,

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:02.910
we cannot begin with the present,

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:04.777
we must begin with the past."

00:08:06.009 --> 00:08:07.130
And so we're gonna do a little bit of past

00:08:07.130 --> 00:08:09.850
because the past is what informs

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:13.930
how we deal with the
present and the future.

00:08:13.930 --> 00:08:16.210
So I'm gonna go through a few slides

00:08:17.740 --> 00:08:22.740
with, again, not an
exhaustive history of race

00:08:24.559 --> 00:08:26.470
and the idea of race,

00:08:26.470 --> 00:08:31.100
but just some points
that I think are relevant

00:08:31.100 --> 00:08:33.153
to what we gonna be talking about.

00:08:33.153 --> 00:08:34.200
So in terms of people thinking about,

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:36.200
I guess I could take my mask off, can I?

00:08:37.790 --> 00:08:40.973
That'll be better for
you and for me. (laughs)

00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:46.940
So in thinking about race
and the creation of the idea,

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:51.650
of course, as we know, race
and racism has everything

00:08:51.650 --> 00:08:55.200
to do with people and how
people see each other.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:56.390
So once people,

00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:59.560
and in this case, European,

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:02.940
mostly explorers back in the
15th Century and probably

00:09:04.070 --> 00:09:07.540
before started engaging with
people who looked differently

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:11.310
from them, those ideas
of who are these people?

00:09:11.310 --> 00:09:13.660
Why are they different?
How are they different?

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:15.570
Started to percolate.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:17.500
So we have European Explorers.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:20.240
We have the start of the
Transatlantic Slave Trade,

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:23.868
the movement of people
across the Atlantic,

00:09:23.868 --> 00:09:26.990
from different parts of Africa going

00:09:26.990 --> 00:09:30.370
to other parts of the world.

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:32.630
And so those ideas
started to percolate, but

00:09:33.701 --> 00:09:38.070
in the 18th Century, we had scientists

00:09:38.070 --> 00:09:42.430
also doing their thing in their labs and

00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:44.864
wherever they did them in their homes,

00:09:44.864 --> 00:09:46.610
wherever these ideas came from.

00:09:46.610 --> 00:09:50.790
we had Carolus Linnaeus, Mr. Taxonomy,

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:53.740
who started grouping
humans based on geography

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:57.818
and came up with his
four part grouping there.

00:09:57.818 --> 00:10:01.620
And then later on, we
have Blumenbach who many,

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:06.530
not all credit or discredit
with the creation of this idea

00:10:06.530 --> 00:10:11.187
of race in terms of the
hierarchies that we have.

00:10:11.187 --> 00:10:13.800
And so we have Blumenbach,
his dissertation work,

00:10:13.800 --> 00:10:15.490
and then later publications

00:10:16.740 --> 00:10:21.630
creating these categories and
becoming one of the first,

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:24.678
and there were other people
doing this around that time,

00:10:24.678 --> 00:10:27.150
but he's is one of the key
ones in terms of creating

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:29.870
this hierarchy based on what he viewed

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:33.440
as the most beautiful people
from the caucuses mountains

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:36.710
and putting one of the people

00:10:36.710 --> 00:10:39.093
who came up with the word Caucasian,

00:10:40.272 --> 00:10:43.693
and then putting everybody else below.

00:10:44.641 --> 00:10:46.763
And then we have Thomas Jefferson,

00:10:46.763 --> 00:10:48.240
Third President of the US

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:53.240
being one of the first
prominent US leaders

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:56.450
to actually put forward this idea

00:10:56.450 --> 00:11:01.450
that people who we call black people,

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:08.440
people of African descent
are less than or inferior

00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:13.920
to people who we call in the US

00:11:15.162 --> 00:11:17.223
at least who we call whites.

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:22.060
So one of the first people
to start using these...

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:24.050
You see, he talks about species

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:26.520
and taking from the scientists work

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:29.800
and bringing it into how society starts

00:11:29.800 --> 00:11:32.293
to think about people and difference.

00:11:35.514 --> 00:11:37.510
Then we have the first census in 1790,

00:11:37.510 --> 00:11:40.310
and you may not be able to read this.

00:11:40.310 --> 00:11:41.620
I don't expect you to read all of it,

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:44.246
but what I want you to
see this is created by,

00:11:44.246 --> 00:11:47.550
developed by the census in 2012,

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:50.750
showing how categories,
census categories have changed

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:52.730
since the first census in 1790,

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:55.600
where race was used in the census.

00:11:55.600 --> 00:11:57.635
And you see the categories there,

00:11:57.635 --> 00:12:01.580
and this shows how these
categories have changed over time,

00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:03.410
over time, they have changed.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:05.410
The one category in the census that has

00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:07.110
not changed, doesn't anybody know?

00:12:08.073 --> 00:12:10.050
Which category has not really changed

00:12:10.050 --> 00:12:13.030
over all these years from 1790 to,

00:12:13.030 --> 00:12:15.650
I cut it off 'cause it was
too big so you stopped there.

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:17.090
But if you go to that website,

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:19.970
it's an interactive resource.

00:12:19.970 --> 00:12:22.900
You can click on these and it
gives you all the information,

00:12:22.900 --> 00:12:24.880
click on any of these and
you get more information,

00:12:24.880 --> 00:12:26.870
click on the dates, you
get more inter information

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:28.750
about all of those identities.

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:30.301
Doesn't anybody know?

00:12:30.301 --> 00:12:32.870
Anybody can tell me. Somebody here?

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:35.830
White has not changed.

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:38.620
Who belongs to that
group has changed, right?

00:12:38.620 --> 00:12:41.930
Who can be included and
excluded has changed over time,

00:12:41.930 --> 00:12:44.340
but the category itself has not.

00:12:44.340 --> 00:12:46.890
All these other categories have changed.

00:12:46.890 --> 00:12:50.702
And you see where Chinese came in,

00:12:50.702 --> 00:12:52.100
mostly from immigration,

00:12:52.100 --> 00:12:54.100
we get a lot of information just

00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:56.220
from this graphic.

00:12:56.220 --> 00:12:58.130
And so the census, and I show the census

00:12:58.130 --> 00:13:00.750
and I mentioned the census
because the census is key

00:13:00.750 --> 00:13:02.720
to how we think about race.

00:13:02.720 --> 00:13:05.400
And when we look at the US census today,

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:07.500
the category is that we use in the census

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:12.500
very much mirror Blumenbach's
taxonomies very much.

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:15.010
Now, we're saying we need to move away.

00:13:15.010 --> 00:13:16.560
We don't use Caucasian in the census

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.540
anymore in the US, but we did,

00:13:18.540 --> 00:13:20.563
it was there, it's not there anymore.

00:13:21.444 --> 00:13:22.910
Different countries as we
know have different census.

00:13:22.910 --> 00:13:25.239
And most of what I talk about today,

00:13:25.239 --> 00:13:27.310
'cause we don't have time to
get into the global aspects

00:13:27.310 --> 00:13:30.613
of race which could take us
in some other directions.

00:13:33.091 --> 00:13:34.343
But over time,

00:13:36.505 --> 00:13:37.756
these have changed.

00:13:37.756 --> 00:13:38.650
But the census becomes important

00:13:38.650 --> 00:13:40.100
because we use it in research.

00:13:40.100 --> 00:13:41.510
We use those categories in research,

00:13:41.510 --> 00:13:44.110
we use it in clinical, we
use them in clinical care

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:46.830
and the questions about
why and how and whether

00:13:46.830 --> 00:13:49.380
we ought to use them are
some of the big questions.

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:52.590
And then we have Darwin,
Father of Evolution

00:13:53.483 --> 00:13:54.560
and his writings

00:13:58.696 --> 00:14:00.960
on the Origin of Species
and then Descent of Man.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:02.020
And in Descent of Man,

00:14:02.020 --> 00:14:03.870
he actually became one
of the first people,

00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:06.780
and there are lots of things
we could say about Darwin

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:08.890
and we are not gonna talk
a lot about Darwin today,

00:14:08.890 --> 00:14:12.080
but one of the first prominent scientists

00:14:13.090 --> 00:14:17.313
to question this notion of
the existence of human races.

00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:22.090
Eugenics have cause

00:14:23.743 --> 00:14:26.318
of Darwin,

00:14:26.318 --> 00:14:27.670
Galton actually coined the term

00:14:27.670 --> 00:14:29.320
and brought that home movement in

00:14:30.219 --> 00:14:31.512
and Charles Davenport

00:14:31.512 --> 00:14:33.860
who started the Eugenics
Record Office here in the US

00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:35.910
in 1910

00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:37.510
at Cold Springs Harbor.

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:40.203
So eugenics became a thing and people

00:14:43.053 --> 00:14:45.284
will argue that we still
have eugenics today.

00:14:45.284 --> 00:14:46.410
Not so much argue that we still have it

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:47.680
in all kinds of forms

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:51.570
so eugenics as a movement.

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:52.750
And in the early 19s,

00:14:52.750 --> 00:14:55.536
early 20th Century to
the mid 20th Century,

00:14:55.536 --> 00:14:58.450
there was a series of
debates among anthropologists

00:14:58.450 --> 00:15:03.450
and biologists, Montague
and race and Livingston,

00:15:04.199 --> 00:15:07.770
about just very similar to
those debates we're having

00:15:07.770 --> 00:15:11.370
right now about race and the
existence of race and ethnicity

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:13.460
and what they are and what they're not,

00:15:13.460 --> 00:15:16.040
and what biology says about them.

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:17.690
And then we have Richard Lewontin

00:15:19.200 --> 00:15:23.860
from Harvard who in the
1970s did studies looking

00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:25.560
at blood group antigens

00:15:26.574 --> 00:15:30.810
and looking at populations
across the world.

00:15:30.810 --> 00:15:32.360
And there are all kinds
of things we could say

00:15:32.360 --> 00:15:34.260
about those studies he did in the 70s,

00:15:35.134 --> 00:15:37.440
but his studies were
some of the first ones

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:39.640
to tell us a bit about genetic variation,

00:15:39.640 --> 00:15:41.030
biological variation

00:15:41.910 --> 00:15:43.790
among populations and told us that most

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:46.990
of the genetic variation we
see is within populations

00:15:46.990 --> 00:15:49.950
about 85% or so of genetic variation

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:51.680
can be found within populations

00:15:53.011 --> 00:15:56.653
and very little variation
between populations.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:02.360
And then the human genome
project that confirmed

00:16:02.360 --> 00:16:03.907
some of Lewontin's work

00:16:03.907 --> 00:16:07.460
and some of the previous
work that as humans,

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:10.080
as a human species, we are more alike

00:16:11.004 --> 00:16:13.100
than we're different,

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:16.450
99.9% the same we say as humans.

00:16:16.450 --> 00:16:18.820
And around the time of
the Human Genome Project

00:16:20.244 --> 00:16:22.070
just before it ended and after,

00:16:22.070 --> 00:16:25.430
we had other scientists
that were using different

00:16:25.430 --> 00:16:28.207
kinds of methods, Alan
Templeton in the 90s

00:16:29.726 --> 00:16:33.310
and early 2000s used right statistic,

00:16:33.310 --> 00:16:37.330
which is a methodology of
looking at genetic variation,

00:16:37.330 --> 00:16:39.758
and geneticist population,

00:16:39.758 --> 00:16:41.220
genetics has still used right statistic

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:44.020
and using that statistic as a
measure of genetic variation,

00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:45.670
looking at variation within population,

00:16:45.670 --> 00:16:49.310
within individual groups
and then as a measure of,

00:16:49.310 --> 00:16:51.850
and looking at their in
relation to the globe,

00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:53.420
the broader population

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:55.750
and looking at humans in relation

00:16:55.750 --> 00:16:56.860
to other organisms

00:17:00.197 --> 00:17:03.713
and concluding that humans
have very little variation

00:17:03.713 --> 00:17:06.670
within the group, genetic variation.

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:11.600
So again, right statistic less than 0.25,

00:17:11.600 --> 00:17:13.893
when you look across the human genome,

00:17:13.893 --> 00:17:16.350
we're saying probably
about 0.1, five, six,

00:17:16.350 --> 00:17:17.530
in terms of this particular statistic,

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:20.653
very low variation within the population.

00:17:20.653 --> 00:17:25.653
And the statistic used
generally says, if variation is,

00:17:26.820 --> 00:17:28.770
if the right statistic is below 0.25,

00:17:28.770 --> 00:17:32.270
then we can say humans
don't have biological race.

00:17:32.270 --> 00:17:35.220
And when I say biological race,

00:17:35.220 --> 00:17:38.750
and I know that we have some
evolutionary people in here,

00:17:38.750 --> 00:17:40.120
and there are all kinds

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:44.190
of ideas and theories about
how we think about race,

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:46.280
and for some evolutionary biologists,

00:17:46.280 --> 00:17:49.610
race is synonymous with subspecies,

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:51.010
which is a level of divergence

00:17:51.010 --> 00:17:53.330
below the level of the species.

00:17:53.330 --> 00:17:54.620
And when we look at humans,

00:17:54.620 --> 00:17:59.210
we don't see that level of
divergence in terms of variation.

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:02.730
And so using a biological measure,

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:06.440
we can say humans don't
have biological race.

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:09.500
But what people often say is that

00:18:12.170 --> 00:18:14.713
biological race doesn't exist.

00:18:15.690 --> 00:18:19.250
And many in evolutionary
biology would say, it does,

00:18:19.250 --> 00:18:20.903
it just doesn't exist in humans.

00:18:22.341 --> 00:18:24.030
Many will say for some organisms,

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:26.130
and if you look here,
you'll say, and again,

00:18:26.130 --> 00:18:29.330
we could debate this, but many
will say for some organisms,

00:18:29.330 --> 00:18:31.730
you could say they have
biological race because of the

00:18:31.730 --> 00:18:34.670
amount of variation
within those populations,

00:18:34.670 --> 00:18:37.160
but for humans, we don't.

00:18:37.160 --> 00:18:40.017
And there are other studies
that show, talk about

00:18:40.017 --> 00:18:42.850
that give us information about the origins

00:18:42.850 --> 00:18:45.020
of humans and this one

00:18:49.437 --> 00:18:53.130
by John Novembre and Colleagues
that that confirmed studies

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:56.010
that we had already seen
about the common origin,

00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:58.380
the African origin of humans,

00:18:58.380 --> 00:19:01.080
again, confirming that we are one species,

00:19:01.080 --> 00:19:03.280
one origin, same people.

00:19:03.280 --> 00:19:06.880
We don't have biological
variation to that rises

00:19:06.880 --> 00:19:11.270
to the level of subspecies
or the level of race.

00:19:11.270 --> 00:19:15.020
So we say, there's no
biological race in humans.

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:16.800
And these are four criteria

00:19:16.800 --> 00:19:18.270
and there may be more,

00:19:18.270 --> 00:19:19.937
some may add others to it,

00:19:19.937 --> 00:19:23.790
but these are four criteria
that we often use in genetics

00:19:23.790 --> 00:19:27.360
and some other sciences

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:31.600
to say that humans don't
have biological race.

00:19:31.600 --> 00:19:33.370
We look at geographic variation.

00:19:33.370 --> 00:19:36.500
And when we look at populations globally,

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:40.280
there's no place that we can
say one population starts

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:41.470
and the other one end.

00:19:41.470 --> 00:19:45.440
Variation is overlapping, it's continuous,

00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:47.653
and they're not these discrete boundaries.

00:19:48.740 --> 00:19:51.877
Gene frequency, Lewontin showed us

00:19:51.877 --> 00:19:54.150
and other studies have shown
us more genetic variation

00:19:54.150 --> 00:19:56.130
within groups than between.

00:19:56.130 --> 00:20:00.840
Morphology, which is one of
the big markers that we use

00:20:01.880 --> 00:20:03.273
to define race.

00:20:04.352 --> 00:20:06.313
And I'll talk about
social race in a minute,

00:20:06.313 --> 00:20:07.710
but we used to define race.

00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:12.563
When we look at morphology,
we see non concordance,

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:16.590
what people call kinky
hair doesn't necessarily go

00:20:16.590 --> 00:20:17.710
with dark skin

00:20:19.743 --> 00:20:22.316
or with a broad nose or all these features

00:20:22.316 --> 00:20:23.720
that we tie to races.

00:20:23.720 --> 00:20:26.450
They're not obligatory really linked

00:20:26.450 --> 00:20:29.420
to each other or to disease.

00:20:29.420 --> 00:20:31.193
We can see people with blonde hair,

00:20:32.134 --> 00:20:34.410
blue eyes in Sierra Leone or

00:20:34.410 --> 00:20:39.255
in the Solomon Islands, in
other places from Europe,

00:20:39.255 --> 00:20:40.266
different from Europe.

00:20:40.266 --> 00:20:42.120
And then lineage, as I said before,

00:20:42.120 --> 00:20:44.893
no subspecies,

00:20:48.715 --> 00:20:50.840
no separate lineages within humans,

00:20:50.840 --> 00:20:53.720
it's one lineage, one species.

00:20:53.720 --> 00:20:56.840
So using these criteria, we say,

00:20:56.840 --> 00:20:58.930
humans don't have biological race.

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:01.393
Some people will say, race does not exist.

00:21:02.290 --> 00:21:04.931
Race does not exist in humans,

00:21:04.931 --> 00:21:05.830
but that's not exactly true.

00:21:05.830 --> 00:21:08.110
We don't have biological race based

00:21:08.110 --> 00:21:09.810
on these data that I just present,

00:21:11.276 --> 00:21:13.000
but social race exists
'cause we've created it.

00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:18.000
It exists and it has real
implications on people's lives.

00:21:18.270 --> 00:21:20.810
The problem is that we
conflate those two things

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:23.980
which is the reason for the seminar today.

00:21:23.980 --> 00:21:25.090
It's like, what is race?

00:21:25.090 --> 00:21:27.520
Is it biological? Is it social?

00:21:27.520 --> 00:21:28.600
How do we distinguish those two things?

00:21:28.600 --> 00:21:31.730
And how do we resolve the
complications that come

00:21:31.730 --> 00:21:33.450
from linking those two things.

00:21:33.450 --> 00:21:37.430
So saying biological race
exists, but not in humans.

00:21:37.430 --> 00:21:42.060
Socially defined or socially
constructed race is real,

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:45.990
we've created it and it has
impacts on people's lives,

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:50.290
including biological
impacts and influences.

00:21:50.290 --> 00:21:53.380
So distinguishing those
two things is critical

00:21:53.380 --> 00:21:55.380
as we think about race.

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:57.673
what does biology say about race?

00:21:58.732 --> 00:21:59.565
What do humans have to say about

00:21:59.565 --> 00:22:01.853
what we've created that we call race?

00:22:05.011 --> 00:22:08.703
So as I said before, have
become as more I study race,

00:22:08.703 --> 00:22:13.067
it's more, I have become interested

00:22:13.067 --> 00:22:18.067
in getting real empirical
data about what people think

00:22:18.310 --> 00:22:21.050
and people who will use this information,

00:22:21.050 --> 00:22:24.150
people who are using
and developing studies.

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:25.970
And so some colleagues and I, colleagues

00:22:25.970 --> 00:22:27.940
at University of Washington
like (indistinct)

00:22:27.940 --> 00:22:31.320
and others did a study a few years ago,

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:34.770
looking at anthropologists
and geneticists,

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:37.820
anthropology professionals
and genetics professionals.

00:22:37.820 --> 00:22:40.130
And we surveyed large numbers.

00:22:40.130 --> 00:22:43.610
And you see here where we started,

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:46.980
who was invited and then
we got for geneticists,

00:22:46.980 --> 00:22:51.830
we have 1,500, anthropologists,
we invited for 4,000,

00:22:51.830 --> 00:22:55.030
we ended up with 300, 3,000 and something

00:22:55.030 --> 00:22:56.680
and then written comments.

00:22:56.680 --> 00:22:58.960
So I'm gonna just show you
some of the data that we got

00:22:58.960 --> 00:23:01.900
and then some of the quantitative
as well as qualitative.

00:23:01.900 --> 00:23:03.623
And we've published these studies.

00:23:05.060 --> 00:23:06.080
One of them is still in progress in terms

00:23:06.080 --> 00:23:08.880
of the quantitative study for geneticists.

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:11.010
But I'm gonna show you
some data that compare

00:23:11.010 --> 00:23:14.170
the perspectives between
geneticists and anthropologists.

00:23:14.170 --> 00:23:18.183
And I showed you those four
criteria that we use to say,

00:23:22.777 --> 00:23:25.630
humans don't have biological race.

00:23:25.630 --> 00:23:29.360
But as we know in the
field, and I'm a geneticist,

00:23:29.360 --> 00:23:32.043
I know within the field of human genetics,

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:35.890
even though we say there is consensus,

00:23:35.890 --> 00:23:37.490
there are differences of opinion

00:23:38.505 --> 00:23:40.150
and these data showed us that.

00:23:40.150 --> 00:23:44.220
And so these are some of those data.

00:23:44.220 --> 00:23:46.340
So what we did, Mike and I,

00:23:46.340 --> 00:23:49.740
we picked some statements that
we call common statements.

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:52.240
And these are statements
that people make all the time

00:23:52.240 --> 00:23:55.460
and say, they'll say,
geneticists agree on this

00:23:55.460 --> 00:23:57.041
or don't agree on this.

00:23:57.041 --> 00:23:59.320
And these are some of
these, races don't exist,

00:23:59.320 --> 00:24:01.530
race has no biological basis,

00:24:01.530 --> 00:24:04.726
race is biological,
meaningless, dah, dah, dah.

00:24:04.726 --> 00:24:05.763
As we asked this,

00:24:05.763 --> 00:24:06.596
we and the surveys were the same for

00:24:06.596 --> 00:24:09.086
both the geneticists
and the anthropologists,

00:24:09.086 --> 00:24:11.460
but just changing them
accordingly in terms of

00:24:11.460 --> 00:24:13.500
when we asked them about their disciplines

00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:15.030
and all of that, that's the only part

00:24:15.030 --> 00:24:16.870
that was different in
terms of demographics,

00:24:16.870 --> 00:24:18.874
but everything was the same.

00:24:18.874 --> 00:24:20.135
What do you notice? Anybody?

00:24:20.135 --> 00:24:21.263
What do you notice when
you look at these data?

00:24:24.660 --> 00:24:25.493
Huh?

00:24:25.493 --> 00:24:28.373
Exact opposite, right? (laughs)

00:24:30.470 --> 00:24:33.320
I will say that most
of the anthropologists

00:24:33.320 --> 00:24:36.940
were cultural anthropologists,
which might not surprise us.

00:24:36.940 --> 00:24:41.640
The geneticists were
mostly clinical geneticists

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:42.780
and genetic counselors,

00:24:42.780 --> 00:24:44.340
but we had molecular geneticists,

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:47.070
population geneticists as well.

00:24:47.070 --> 00:24:49.010
So that might have something
to do what we are seeing,

00:24:49.010 --> 00:24:52.433
but what we see here is
exact opposite responses.

00:24:56.407 --> 00:24:58.080
I'm just gonna show some
of the quantitative data,

00:24:58.080 --> 00:25:00.919
qualitative data, I may
not be able to show,

00:25:00.919 --> 00:25:01.752
I have a bunch of quotes.

00:25:01.752 --> 00:25:02.687
This came from a geneticist,

00:25:02.687 --> 00:25:05.420
"Geneticists who say that race
doesn't exist are in denial.

00:25:05.420 --> 00:25:07.170
Obviously they have never
looked at the results

00:25:07.170 --> 00:25:09.590
of an AIMS, ancestry informative markers,

00:25:09.590 --> 00:25:12.970
that's what that is, Panel
on 1,000 African Americans

00:25:12.970 --> 00:25:15.483
and 1,000 European Caucasians.

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:20.130
And clearly the outmoded
racial identifiers work

00:25:21.049 --> 00:25:21.930
more often than it fails.

00:25:21.930 --> 00:25:24.920
That's the science, regardless
of anyone's opinion."

00:25:24.920 --> 00:25:25.920
That's a geneticist.

00:25:26.865 --> 00:25:29.560
We asked the question
and this is something

00:25:29.560 --> 00:25:30.730
I've long been interested in,

00:25:30.730 --> 00:25:32.520
and I've actually written on this

00:25:32.520 --> 00:25:34.070
that we should replace race.

00:25:34.070 --> 00:25:37.263
I've rethought that I
wrote that paper in 2006.

00:25:37.263 --> 00:25:39.769
I have some thoughts about changing that.

00:25:39.769 --> 00:25:42.200
I still think we need to get rid of it,

00:25:42.200 --> 00:25:44.727
but I think some things
I wrote in that paper,

00:25:44.727 --> 00:25:46.640
I might have said differently.

00:25:46.640 --> 00:25:49.140
But the question of whether
we need to do away with race

00:25:49.140 --> 00:25:50.350
and replace it with something else.

00:25:50.350 --> 00:25:52.837
So these were some of
the questions we asked.

00:25:52.837 --> 00:25:55.770
And in general, you see
that there's some agreement

00:25:55.770 --> 00:26:00.437
that we need to come up with
something else apart from race.

00:26:00.437 --> 00:26:05.293
Well, as work has moved
forward over the years,

00:26:06.300 --> 00:26:10.060
there are those who have
been asking and wondering

00:26:10.060 --> 00:26:13.070
whether we are asking the right questions.

00:26:13.070 --> 00:26:15.650
We're asking use race, don't use race

00:26:15.650 --> 00:26:17.720
as if those are the only two options.

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:20.080
There may be questions
that we haven't asked.

00:26:20.080 --> 00:26:21.740
When should we use race?

00:26:21.740 --> 00:26:24.310
Why? How?

00:26:24.310 --> 00:26:26.470
Those are the probably
some of the questions

00:26:26.470 --> 00:26:27.420
that we need to ask

00:26:28.550 --> 00:26:33.170
instead of dichotomizing
it the way we have, but

00:26:33.170 --> 00:26:35.863
I'm not quite there yet,
but I'm getting there.

00:26:38.857 --> 00:26:41.000
"Race is a four letter word
that needs to be retired

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:44.720
and removed from the
dictionary and common use.

00:26:44.720 --> 00:26:46.390
As an anthropologist and human geneticist,

00:26:46.390 --> 00:26:48.810
I have watched as modern
methods of measuring variation

00:26:48.810 --> 00:26:52.630
in humans have made the word
a 19th Century anachronism

00:26:52.630 --> 00:26:55.500
with no scientific value."

00:26:55.500 --> 00:26:57.350
I'm gonna jump over to some of these.

00:27:00.760 --> 00:27:05.440
The question of how we take
this information to healthcare.

00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:08.280
These based on how we think about race,

00:27:08.280 --> 00:27:10.812
the knowledge, the information we know

00:27:10.812 --> 00:27:11.950
that biological race does not exist,

00:27:11.950 --> 00:27:14.326
but we see these health differences,

00:27:14.326 --> 00:27:16.450
how do we reconcile those things?

00:27:16.450 --> 00:27:18.370
And then there was
somebody who's just like,

00:27:18.370 --> 00:27:22.320
forget this human business. (laughs)

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:24.456
I just don't have to deal with this,

00:27:24.456 --> 00:27:26.480
forgetting that they're part of society

00:27:26.480 --> 00:27:28.080
and they gotta deal with it too.

00:27:28.940 --> 00:27:32.530
So in thinking about looking
at the general population,

00:27:32.530 --> 00:27:36.370
So we did a surveys
colleagues at UNC Chapel Hill

00:27:36.370 --> 00:27:39.260
and myself and colleagues
at Duke did a survey,

00:27:39.260 --> 00:27:42.410
a national survey of the US population.

00:27:42.410 --> 00:27:45.000
We had about 1700 people,

00:27:45.000 --> 00:27:48.683
a representative sample of
the US population on race.

00:27:50.070 --> 00:27:51.620
And this was one question

00:27:51.620 --> 00:27:54.360
one of my undergrads is working on this,

00:27:54.360 --> 00:27:57.780
looking at people's perceptions

00:27:58.660 --> 00:28:01.350
of the differences between
race, ethnicity, and ancestry.

00:28:01.350 --> 00:28:03.500
This is a qualitative question,

00:28:03.500 --> 00:28:05.423
open-ended question and so we coded,

00:28:06.345 --> 00:28:07.210
and these are the responses

00:28:08.410 --> 00:28:12.507
we got in terms of the
definitions of those words.

00:28:12.507 --> 00:28:14.790
"Cause as you know, these
terms are used interchangeably

00:28:15.788 --> 00:28:18.465
and we still are so confused
about how we distinguish them,

00:28:18.465 --> 00:28:19.298
but that's not our conversation today.

00:28:19.298 --> 00:28:21.126
We're gonna focus on race,

00:28:21.126 --> 00:28:22.110
But anybody can tell me quickly,

00:28:22.110 --> 00:28:24.120
what is one thing you notice

00:28:24.120 --> 00:28:26.203
on that bar graph?

00:28:32.120 --> 00:28:33.873
One thing that strikes you on that.

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:38.450
- [Participant] Physical
geographical (indistinct)

00:28:38.450 --> 00:28:39.400
- [Royal] They are.

00:28:40.356 --> 00:28:41.720
(indistinct)

00:28:41.720 --> 00:28:42.900
That's right, yes.

00:28:42.900 --> 00:28:45.210
That is definitely one of the things

00:28:45.210 --> 00:28:49.410
that is striking in this on this.

00:28:49.410 --> 00:28:50.913
Anybody else? Anything else?

00:28:52.546 --> 00:28:57.546
- [Participant 2] (indistinct)
Very positive (indistinct)

00:29:00.597 --> 00:29:02.542
(audience laughing)

00:29:02.542 --> 00:29:04.223
- [Royal] And what did most
of them say they don't know?

00:29:06.110 --> 00:29:09.310
Ethnicity, right? Which
we found interesting.

00:29:09.310 --> 00:29:12.450
Most people did not know
how to define ethnicity.

00:29:12.450 --> 00:29:13.930
They could tell you something about race,

00:29:13.930 --> 00:29:15.370
whether it was correct or not,

00:29:15.370 --> 00:29:18.267
they could give you a definition for race.

00:29:18.267 --> 00:29:20.330
And when we asked them about their race,

00:29:20.330 --> 00:29:21.163
they could tell us something.

00:29:21.163 --> 00:29:25.070
When we asked people about
their ethnicity, I don't know,

00:29:25.070 --> 00:29:26.120
they give you their race.

00:29:26.120 --> 00:29:28.448
Mostly Hispanic people would say,

00:29:28.448 --> 00:29:30.730
they use race and ethnicity,

00:29:30.730 --> 00:29:33.310
many of the Hispanics in
the study, the same thing,

00:29:33.310 --> 00:29:36.340
but most people, and even
we did that in a pilot

00:29:36.340 --> 00:29:38.135
and it's the same thing.

00:29:38.135 --> 00:29:40.400
Most people have no
clue what ethnicity is,

00:29:40.400 --> 00:29:43.423
whether how to define it or
what their ethnicity might be.

00:29:44.875 --> 00:29:48.743
And that's something we're
following up on more.

00:29:49.740 --> 00:29:53.370
We also see that for genetic
and we could look at genetic

00:29:53.370 --> 00:29:57.090
and physical as similar,
but for genetic, of course,

00:29:57.090 --> 00:29:59.370
race more likely,

00:29:59.370 --> 00:30:04.270
most people were more likely
to link race and genetics.

00:30:04.270 --> 00:30:06.330
When we ask the question
about the existence

00:30:06.330 --> 00:30:10.410
of biological human races,
this is what we saw.

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:14.533
So we have gender left,
right black, white.

00:30:15.460 --> 00:30:18.530
And what we see and the
line going across is a mean

00:30:18.530 --> 00:30:19.890
for all the other groups,

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:21.700
blacks and white were our largest groups,

00:30:21.700 --> 00:30:22.960
the other groups were small

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:26.870
so we did them in terms
of what they're showing us

00:30:26.870 --> 00:30:28.180
on these graphs.

00:30:28.180 --> 00:30:30.545
And we see that education,

00:30:30.545 --> 00:30:34.653
people with with less education
were more likely to think

00:30:34.653 --> 00:30:38.480
that whether they were men identified

00:30:39.811 --> 00:30:41.650
as men or women were more likely

00:30:41.650 --> 00:30:44.060
to think that biological races exist.

00:30:44.060 --> 00:30:47.718
We found that people identified
as white were more likely

00:30:47.718 --> 00:30:52.073
to think that biological races exist.

00:30:56.470 --> 00:30:59.320
We have a scale that we are
in the process of validating.

00:31:00.250 --> 00:31:01.920
And we'd used this scale in this.

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:05.520
And on that scale, looking
at race and genetics,

00:31:05.520 --> 00:31:08.180
asking people various things
about the relationship

00:31:08.180 --> 00:31:10.930
between race and genetics,
and this is how they scored.

00:31:11.950 --> 00:31:15.010
And people who were black were
more likely to score higher

00:31:15.010 --> 00:31:19.750
on that scale than people
identified as white.

00:31:19.750 --> 00:31:23.330
Younger people were more
likely to score higher,

00:31:23.330 --> 00:31:25.783
which may not necessarily
be very surprising.

00:31:30.897 --> 00:31:32.530
And yeah, just some of the findings there.

00:31:32.530 --> 00:31:36.463
When we ask people for their comments,

00:31:37.510 --> 00:31:38.510
there are people who

00:31:39.920 --> 00:31:43.240
are thinking race needs
to go, so much baggage,

00:31:43.240 --> 00:31:44.760
people who didn't even know what to say

00:31:44.760 --> 00:31:46.030
because there is so much

00:31:48.030 --> 00:31:48.863
that they could think of.

00:31:48.863 --> 00:31:51.130
Many people said they just never

00:31:51.130 --> 00:31:53.280
even thought about these differences

00:31:57.356 --> 00:31:58.556
and wanted to know more.

00:31:59.895 --> 00:32:01.750
And then just like our
other is complicated,

00:32:01.750 --> 00:32:04.370
it's complicated and we
know it's complicated.

00:32:04.370 --> 00:32:07.590
And it's this complexity that has led me

00:32:07.590 --> 00:32:09.940
to do some of the work I do.

00:32:09.940 --> 00:32:13.290
I've become very interested in complexity.

00:32:13.290 --> 00:32:14.123
And this is

00:32:16.281 --> 00:32:18.836
a model that colleagues and I developed

00:32:18.836 --> 00:32:21.406
and published in 2009.

00:32:21.406 --> 00:32:22.239
This is when I was at Howard

00:32:22.239 --> 00:32:24.253
and this was part of an NIH grant we had.

00:32:25.570 --> 00:32:27.180
And what we did

00:32:29.020 --> 00:32:32.683
in this model is utilize
data mainly most of the data

00:32:32.683 --> 00:32:35.720
were data related to African Americans

00:32:37.170 --> 00:32:41.170
and looking at variables
that influence health

00:32:41.170 --> 00:32:44.470
and interactions, and came
up with this model looking

00:32:44.470 --> 00:32:49.470
at various domains in terms of,

00:32:50.110 --> 00:32:51.860
we looked at genetics, we looked at

00:32:53.440 --> 00:32:56.310
where people are, their identities,

00:32:56.310 --> 00:33:00.723
their social and environmental exposures,

00:33:01.960 --> 00:33:04.020
their psychological...

00:33:07.392 --> 00:33:08.225
My cursor all over the place.

00:33:08.225 --> 00:33:10.030
But anyway, we looked at a range

00:33:11.030 --> 00:33:14.260
of variables here and their
influences on outcomes,

00:33:14.260 --> 00:33:16.860
not just status of
health, but drug response,

00:33:16.860 --> 00:33:18.700
health perception, a range

00:33:18.700 --> 00:33:23.460
of responses informed by data

00:33:26.240 --> 00:33:27.073
in that literature.

00:33:27.073 --> 00:33:30.003
And in that paper in 2009,

00:33:31.460 --> 00:33:34.250
we put out the idea,

00:33:34.250 --> 00:33:36.780
which is an idea I've had for a long time

00:33:36.780 --> 00:33:39.540
about sickle cell disease and looking

00:33:39.540 --> 00:33:41.860
at sickle cell disease as a model

00:33:41.860 --> 00:33:44.950
to help us understand complexity,

00:33:44.950 --> 00:33:46.330
gene environment interactions.

00:33:46.330 --> 00:33:47.580
Now, sickle cell disease,

00:33:48.780 --> 00:33:49.790
I'm gonna just do a little bit for those

00:33:49.790 --> 00:33:52.483
who may not know about
sickle cell disease.

00:33:54.580 --> 00:33:58.660
First, the first known
publication, at least in the US,

00:33:58.660 --> 00:34:00.570
when I talked to my African
colleagues, they're like,

00:34:00.570 --> 00:34:01.960
we've been talking about
this thing for a long time,

00:34:01.960 --> 00:34:03.760
we just didn't know what to call it.

00:34:07.111 --> 00:34:08.670
we didn't have a journal to publish it in.

00:34:08.670 --> 00:34:12.642
And so we say 1910 was the
First published Case in the US

00:34:12.642 --> 00:34:16.420
on sickle cell disease published

00:34:16.420 --> 00:34:20.210
by James Herrick who
identified the first person,

00:34:20.210 --> 00:34:23.630
first person identified
Walter Clement Noel in the US

00:34:24.630 --> 00:34:25.990
in Chicago.

00:34:25.990 --> 00:34:29.860
He was a Grenada dental student in Chicago

00:34:29.860 --> 00:34:31.110
at University of Chicago

00:34:32.359 --> 00:34:33.600
and became ill

00:34:33.600 --> 00:34:37.017
and they found these elongated cells

00:34:37.017 --> 00:34:38.853
and they were published.

00:34:39.921 --> 00:34:41.900
And over time, we've learned a lot

00:34:41.900 --> 00:34:43.040
about sickle cell disease.

00:34:43.040 --> 00:34:46.330
in 1949, Linus Pauling and others told us

00:34:46.330 --> 00:34:48.810
about the molecular basis
of sickle cell disease

00:34:48.810 --> 00:34:52.610
that is a point mutation
in the hemoglobin gene.

00:34:52.610 --> 00:34:56.067
And you see here, this scheme shows

00:34:56.067 --> 00:34:59.880
what we call normal hemoglobin.

00:34:59.880 --> 00:35:01.940
We're careful about how
we use these words normal,

00:35:01.940 --> 00:35:05.490
but we're just gonna
use it today. (chuckles)

00:35:05.490 --> 00:35:08.030
And then sickle, and it's one change,

00:35:08.030 --> 00:35:13.030
one base sphere change in the genetic code

00:35:13.640 --> 00:35:17.660
from a T to an A that gives
us a different amino acid

00:35:17.660 --> 00:35:20.640
from glutamic acid to valine

00:35:20.640 --> 00:35:21.540
and the production of

00:35:21.540 --> 00:35:26.540
this different amino acid
gives us hemoglobin S,

00:35:27.720 --> 00:35:29.930
which is a sickle hemoglobin.

00:35:29.930 --> 00:35:32.750
Normal hemoglobin, that one on the left,

00:35:32.750 --> 00:35:35.110
the red cells are pliable and soft,

00:35:35.110 --> 00:35:37.571
and they can slide through the vesicles,

00:35:37.571 --> 00:35:39.130
the small veins and capillaries.

00:35:39.130 --> 00:35:41.770
The sickle cells, however, become rigid

00:35:41.770 --> 00:35:44.170
because they've lost oxygen, the result of

00:35:46.012 --> 00:35:48.750
of the valine produces a
decrease in amount of oxygen,

00:35:48.750 --> 00:35:51.270
cells become sticky, they clump together

00:35:51.270 --> 00:35:53.523
and going through cells, vesicles,

00:35:54.450 --> 00:35:57.530
capillaries, and veins
cause a lot of pain.

00:35:57.530 --> 00:36:00.760
They cause blockage, what
we call vasal occlusion,

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:03.853
which is part of the
hallmark of the disease.

00:36:04.720 --> 00:36:09.720
We also learned in the 1950s
from AC Allison about the link

00:36:11.070 --> 00:36:15.950
between sickle hemoglobin and malaria.

00:36:15.950 --> 00:36:20.950
And what you see here on
the left is historically

00:36:21.130 --> 00:36:26.130
where malaria was and on
the right sickle cell trait,

00:36:26.440 --> 00:36:27.480
sickle cell trait.

00:36:27.480 --> 00:36:29.400
We also learned, I didn't say before,

00:36:29.400 --> 00:36:31.430
the inheritance of sickle cell disease

00:36:31.430 --> 00:36:33.360
is an autosomal recessive disorder.

00:36:33.360 --> 00:36:36.140
So both parents have to have a trait

00:36:36.140 --> 00:36:38.090
and when they have a
child with every child

00:36:38.090 --> 00:36:39.240
that those parents have,

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:41.250
if each of them has a
trait, with every child,

00:36:41.250 --> 00:36:43.070
there's a 25% chance

00:36:43.070 --> 00:36:45.370
that they could have a
child with the disease.

00:36:47.100 --> 00:36:49.840
And it's the trait that
is linked to malaria.

00:36:49.840 --> 00:36:51.970
And then when people have
come together with a trait,

00:36:51.970 --> 00:36:54.684
then they could have a
child with a disease.

00:36:54.684 --> 00:36:56.390
So the presence of sickle cell trait.

00:36:56.390 --> 00:36:59.610
Sickle cell trait arose as
part of natural selection

00:36:59.610 --> 00:37:02.743
to protect people from
death, from malaria.

00:37:03.616 --> 00:37:06.870
So classic case of natural selection.

00:37:06.870 --> 00:37:08.850
And here we see where malaria is today,

00:37:08.850 --> 00:37:13.465
still mostly in Africa and
some parts of South America.

00:37:13.465 --> 00:37:16.003
But for most part of the
world, it does not exist.

00:37:16.903 --> 00:37:19.320
Sickle cell disease is also probably,

00:37:19.320 --> 00:37:21.810
probably the most racialized disease.

00:37:21.810 --> 00:37:24.136
There are other diseases
that are racialized,

00:37:24.136 --> 00:37:26.270
but sickle cell disease has been.

00:37:26.270 --> 00:37:28.179
And this perception
that sickle cell disease

00:37:28.179 --> 00:37:29.079
is a black disease

00:37:30.560 --> 00:37:34.460
has been part of the problem that people

00:37:34.460 --> 00:37:36.960
with the disease have in
terms of their treatment.

00:37:38.734 --> 00:37:40.760
And part of my interest,

00:37:40.760 --> 00:37:43.400
I worked on sickle cell
disease in my graduate work

00:37:43.400 --> 00:37:46.190
and as a genetic counselor
at a Sickle Cell Center

00:37:46.190 --> 00:37:49.130
and got interested in very,
more interested in diseases.

00:37:49.130 --> 00:37:50.200
I saw families

00:37:52.452 --> 00:37:55.110
with children with sickle cell disease

00:37:55.110 --> 00:37:56.720
and the children I had,

00:37:56.720 --> 00:37:59.060
I remember one family with
five children affected

00:37:59.060 --> 00:38:00.621
with sickle cell disease.

00:38:00.621 --> 00:38:01.690
And each one of them was different

00:38:01.690 --> 00:38:02.850
in terms of their outcomes.

00:38:02.850 --> 00:38:05.160
One child was pretty much symptom free.

00:38:05.160 --> 00:38:07.550
You wouldn't even know that
they had sickle cell disease

00:38:07.550 --> 00:38:09.780
and then another one in
the hospital all the time

00:38:09.780 --> 00:38:11.640
and then another one died early.

00:38:11.640 --> 00:38:14.100
And that variability struck me

00:38:14.100 --> 00:38:15.900
and I thought there's gotta be...

00:38:16.909 --> 00:38:17.742
And in grad school, I learned

00:38:17.742 --> 00:38:19.450
about the genetic factors
that influenced that

00:38:19.450 --> 00:38:23.020
but I was also interested in
what are the other things?

00:38:23.020 --> 00:38:26.420
So the racialization of sickle
cell disease is a big part

00:38:26.420 --> 00:38:28.260
of the story of the disease.

00:38:28.260 --> 00:38:30.380
We can't talk about it
without the racialization.

00:38:30.380 --> 00:38:33.260
So sickle cell disease affects
just about every organ.

00:38:33.260 --> 00:38:37.390
Every organ system is
affected and again is affected

00:38:37.390 --> 00:38:40.170
in some people, some people
don't have a lot of symptoms,

00:38:40.170 --> 00:38:42.650
but most people will
have some from your eye,

00:38:42.650 --> 00:38:46.293
all the way to your toes can
be affected as you see there.

00:38:48.560 --> 00:38:50.380
It's also global.

00:38:50.380 --> 00:38:52.770
There are about 20 million people globally

00:38:52.770 --> 00:38:53.950
with sickle cell disease

00:38:53.950 --> 00:38:57.230
most of them are in three countries

00:38:57.230 --> 00:39:02.020
in the Congo, Republic of the
Congo, Nigeria, and India.

00:39:02.020 --> 00:39:04.993
Most of them are in Africa.

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:08.870
And we see the numbers there

00:39:08.870 --> 00:39:13.870
and mostly countries below
the Maghreb Countries

00:39:16.990 --> 00:39:18.600
in Africa.

00:39:18.600 --> 00:39:21.840
And so we see sickle cell
disease as a global disease.

00:39:21.840 --> 00:39:25.583
In the US, about 100,000
people with the disease.

00:39:26.720 --> 00:39:29.914
We see a lot of inequities
in sickle cell disease.

00:39:29.914 --> 00:39:31.530
And many people don't
think about the inequities

00:39:31.530 --> 00:39:33.280
and the disparities in sickle cell disease

00:39:33.280 --> 00:39:34.500
'cause many people say,

00:39:34.500 --> 00:39:36.380
well, we know it's...

00:39:36.380 --> 00:39:38.500
Well, some people think
of it as a black disease.

00:39:38.500 --> 00:39:40.520
Some people say, we know
it's not a black disease,

00:39:40.520 --> 00:39:43.920
but mostly black people
have it, which is true.

00:39:43.920 --> 00:39:48.186
Mostly people who identify as
African, at least in the US,

00:39:48.186 --> 00:39:49.460
but the disease is also
very common in Greece.

00:39:49.460 --> 00:39:51.298
There are some parts of Greece

00:39:51.298 --> 00:39:52.131
that have more sickle cell disease

00:39:52.131 --> 00:39:54.150
than some parts of Africa, South Africa,

00:39:54.150 --> 00:39:56.430
for example, doesn't really
have sickle cell disease.

00:39:56.430 --> 00:39:57.700
Most of the people there are people

00:39:57.700 --> 00:39:59.973
who come from other
parts of the continent.

00:40:01.139 --> 00:40:03.660
And so when we think about
sickle cell disease, it's global,

00:40:03.660 --> 00:40:08.660
Turkey, Middle East, India,
very, very much global.

00:40:09.690 --> 00:40:12.754
But in general, both here in the US

00:40:12.754 --> 00:40:14.620
and globally has received
little attention compared

00:40:14.620 --> 00:40:16.820
to other diseases in terms of funding

00:40:16.820 --> 00:40:18.253
and other things.

00:40:19.390 --> 00:40:22.460
And this is produced
by the CDC Foundation,

00:40:22.460 --> 00:40:25.783
showing some of the disparities
in terms of life expectancy,

00:40:27.400 --> 00:40:28.390
returning to the hospital,
I talked about pain,

00:40:28.390 --> 00:40:30.600
the hallmark of the disease,

00:40:30.600 --> 00:40:33.940
more likely even when you
compare sickle cell disease with

00:40:33.940 --> 00:40:37.906
other diseases that have
pain associated with them,

00:40:37.906 --> 00:40:40.050
there's studies, there was
a study done by colleagues

00:40:40.050 --> 00:40:43.130
at Hopkins looking at patients
with long bone fracture.

00:40:43.130 --> 00:40:45.436
And what happens very often is that people

00:40:45.436 --> 00:40:46.269
with sickle cell disease,

00:40:46.269 --> 00:40:48.140
because they suffer from such severe pain,

00:40:48.140 --> 00:40:50.410
have to take narcotics for the most part

00:40:50.410 --> 00:40:52.190
to deal with the pain

00:40:52.190 --> 00:40:54.610
and they're always in
the ER for the most part.

00:40:54.610 --> 00:40:57.783
And very often they,

00:40:59.490 --> 00:41:01.113
as we go down here,

00:41:02.092 --> 00:41:03.190
I'm not gonna get to go
through all of these.

00:41:03.190 --> 00:41:07.880
But patients are often
seen as drug seeking

00:41:08.820 --> 00:41:09.920
and in the US, as we said,

00:41:09.920 --> 00:41:11.520
most of the patients are patients

00:41:11.520 --> 00:41:13.700
who identify as African American.

00:41:13.700 --> 00:41:15.360
There are many patients who identify

00:41:15.360 --> 00:41:17.770
as Hispanic too with sickle cell disease.

00:41:17.770 --> 00:41:19.840
But that is one key component

00:41:19.840 --> 00:41:22.880
of sickle cell disease
experience that people have.

00:41:22.880 --> 00:41:25.860
And I'm interested in knowing
do people across the world in

00:41:25.860 --> 00:41:28.030
other countries have that experience

00:41:28.030 --> 00:41:30.250
as I think about race
and how it plays out.

00:41:30.250 --> 00:41:32.575
And then we also know that the number

00:41:32.575 --> 00:41:33.480
of physicians who are
willing to see people

00:41:33.480 --> 00:41:36.660
with sickle cell disease
is also a small number.

00:41:36.660 --> 00:41:39.870
Some people just don't
wanna deal with them.

00:41:39.870 --> 00:41:42.110
Now in thinking about factors

00:41:42.110 --> 00:41:43.370
that influence sickle cell disease,

00:41:43.370 --> 00:41:45.990
we've long known that
there are genetic factors.

00:41:45.990 --> 00:41:49.555
It's a classic Mendelian
single gene disorder.

00:41:49.555 --> 00:41:53.850
You can't get it unless you
inherit those those variants

00:41:53.850 --> 00:41:55.790
from both of your parents.

00:41:55.790 --> 00:41:57.870
And so there are these genetic factors,

00:41:57.870 --> 00:41:59.510
the hemoglobin genotype,

00:41:59.510 --> 00:42:01.190
whether you're SS there're about,

00:42:01.190 --> 00:42:04.095
there're over 1,000 hemoglobin variants,

00:42:04.095 --> 00:42:04.928
S is the most severe

00:42:04.928 --> 00:42:06.120
and the one we talk about here,

00:42:06.120 --> 00:42:09.350
but there's C as well and S
with another abnormal hemoglobin

00:42:09.350 --> 00:42:11.450
will result in sickle cell disease.

00:42:11.450 --> 00:42:12.990
But the genotype, the haplotype

00:42:12.990 --> 00:42:14.860
and all these are genetic factors.

00:42:14.860 --> 00:42:18.130
But less well known
are non-genetic factors

00:42:18.130 --> 00:42:20.034
that influence sickle cell disease.

00:42:20.034 --> 00:42:21.680
And these are some of them, climate,

00:42:21.680 --> 00:42:24.150
all aspects of climate

00:42:24.150 --> 00:42:28.530
and I'm gonna try to wrap up soon (laughs)

00:42:28.530 --> 00:42:29.750
that influenced sickle cell disease,

00:42:29.750 --> 00:42:30.970
but all of these,

00:42:30.970 --> 00:42:32.910
and there are studies that have looked

00:42:34.531 --> 00:42:36.170
at these factors.

00:42:36.170 --> 00:42:38.500
And this is a paper
published by Piel and others

00:42:38.500 --> 00:42:40.100
from the UK where

00:42:41.220 --> 00:42:45.320
they created this image of
some of the genetic modifiers,

00:42:45.320 --> 00:42:47.440
the non-genetic modifiers.

00:42:47.440 --> 00:42:49.530
But what I found interesting
in their paper is

00:42:49.530 --> 00:42:51.550
that they never brought 'em together.

00:42:51.550 --> 00:42:53.910
So we're looking at the
genetic modifiers up here.

00:42:53.910 --> 00:42:57.120
We're looking at the
environmental modifiers down here.

00:42:57.120 --> 00:42:58.450
I have long been interested in bringing

00:42:58.450 --> 00:42:59.840
all of those things together.

00:42:59.840 --> 00:43:01.620
And this is an image from a paper

00:43:01.620 --> 00:43:03.280
that we published last year

00:43:04.500 --> 00:43:06.193
that has seven domains.

00:43:07.527 --> 00:43:09.450
And as you notice, there are
no no boundaries between those,

00:43:09.450 --> 00:43:10.600
because what we know is that all

00:43:10.600 --> 00:43:13.100
of these things are operating together

00:43:13.100 --> 00:43:16.070
at the same time and
understanding that complexity,

00:43:16.070 --> 00:43:18.280
it's challenging, but it's a must

00:43:18.280 --> 00:43:21.610
if we really wanna understand
the mechanisms of the disease.

00:43:21.610 --> 00:43:24.370
And so the paper we published put forward

00:43:27.240 --> 00:43:29.302
this idea of looking
at sickle cell disease,

00:43:29.302 --> 00:43:30.520
and we used pain as an example.

00:43:30.520 --> 00:43:34.073
All of what you see there is
informed by the literature.

00:43:35.120 --> 00:43:39.930
The arrows are factors
that exacerbate pain,

00:43:39.930 --> 00:43:42.500
the circles are factors
that have been shown

00:43:42.500 --> 00:43:44.911
to ameliorate pain.

00:43:44.911 --> 00:43:46.640
So as we think about health and disease

00:43:46.640 --> 00:43:49.770
and race and racism, thinking about fact,

00:43:49.770 --> 00:43:53.800
not just factors that cause
disease or make disease worse,

00:43:53.800 --> 00:43:58.800
but also factors that can
help have a positive impact on

00:43:59.390 --> 00:44:03.260
on our health and outcomes,
both are equally important.

00:44:03.260 --> 00:44:05.690
And this was a timeline we created looking

00:44:05.690 --> 00:44:08.240
at various factors as they go up and down,

00:44:08.240 --> 00:44:11.660
the pain is going up, going
down as different factors,

00:44:11.660 --> 00:44:14.020
the factors from these domains

00:44:14.020 --> 00:44:16.700
and how people's health is increasing

00:44:16.700 --> 00:44:19.513
as their pain decreases.

00:44:20.370 --> 00:44:23.760
And so this work in terms of
looking at the complexity,

00:44:23.760 --> 00:44:26.090
and I'm gonna move on 'cause I know I've

00:44:26.090 --> 00:44:28.570
gotta wrap up soon

00:44:30.470 --> 00:44:35.410
is something that we we've done in,

00:44:35.410 --> 00:44:37.460
we just did a preliminary study looking

00:44:37.460 --> 00:44:39.400
at people with sickle cell disease

00:44:39.400 --> 00:44:42.135
in Jamaica, Cameroon, and South Africa.

00:44:42.135 --> 00:44:43.379
And well, we did South Africa too,

00:44:43.379 --> 00:44:44.900
but this publication
was focused on Jamaica,

00:44:44.900 --> 00:44:46.143
Cameroon and the US.

00:44:47.180 --> 00:44:48.790
And for various reasons,

00:44:48.790 --> 00:44:50.180
we couldn't do the comparisons.

00:44:50.180 --> 00:44:51.730
We're looking at existing data.

00:44:51.730 --> 00:44:54.320
We wanted to look at gene
environment interactions,

00:44:54.320 --> 00:44:55.490
but for all kinds of reasons,

00:44:55.490 --> 00:44:58.175
some sites didn't have
genetic information,

00:44:58.175 --> 00:44:59.008
some didn't have environmental,

00:44:59.008 --> 00:45:02.400
so we just picked one that had
the most robust set of data,

00:45:02.400 --> 00:45:03.530
and that was Jamaica.

00:45:03.530 --> 00:45:07.180
And what we found, we found associations

00:45:07.180 --> 00:45:08.920
in terms of age, in terms

00:45:09.792 --> 00:45:13.213
of atmospheric temperature,
rainfall, zinc,

00:45:14.990 --> 00:45:19.500
lower zinc was a risk factor
for pain and greater poverty.

00:45:19.500 --> 00:45:22.040
So these were some factors that we found

00:45:22.040 --> 00:45:26.910
that influenced severity of
pain or increased severe pain.

00:45:26.910 --> 00:45:29.930
We're surprised not to find any
differences across genotypes

00:45:29.930 --> 00:45:34.150
because the genotypes
are known to be factors

00:45:34.150 --> 00:45:35.210
that influence severity.

00:45:35.210 --> 00:45:37.190
So there might be some things

00:45:37.190 --> 00:45:40.920
that we need to look at there.

00:45:40.920 --> 00:45:43.470
I'm gonna move very quickly into talking

00:45:44.747 --> 00:45:46.660
about race based medicine.

00:45:46.660 --> 00:45:49.570
And what you saw there
was just the assumptions

00:45:49.570 --> 00:45:51.540
that underlie race based medicine.

00:45:51.540 --> 00:45:53.700
And race based medicine, when
I say race based medicine,

00:45:53.700 --> 00:45:55.720
I'm thinking about
practicing medicine based

00:45:55.720 --> 00:45:58.320
on these racial categories,
how we treat people,

00:45:58.320 --> 00:45:59.860
the drugs that we give them,

00:45:59.860 --> 00:46:02.144
the treatments that we give them

00:46:02.144 --> 00:46:04.640
and the things that we
think will work best

00:46:04.640 --> 00:46:07.140
for them in terms of their health.

00:46:07.140 --> 00:46:09.223
And the assumptions are that races exist.

00:46:10.383 --> 00:46:13.000
And that races, biological races exist,

00:46:13.000 --> 00:46:14.740
that they differ in
their genetic components

00:46:14.740 --> 00:46:17.020
and that these differences influence

00:46:17.020 --> 00:46:19.230
those health outcomes.

00:46:19.230 --> 00:46:21.990
So I'm gonna talk very
quickly about BiDil,

00:46:21.990 --> 00:46:23.960
which is a drug, the first drug approved

00:46:23.960 --> 00:46:27.450
by the FDA to treat a
specific racial group.

00:46:27.450 --> 00:46:29.663
Has everybody in here heard about BiDil?

00:46:30.500 --> 00:46:31.530
Some people have.

00:46:31.530 --> 00:46:34.090
Anybody never, never heard about BiDil?

00:46:34.090 --> 00:46:35.640
Oh, we have some people who
never heard about BiDil?

00:46:35.640 --> 00:46:37.890
We're gonna take a couple
of minutes, just a couple,

00:46:37.890 --> 00:46:40.348
'cause I don't wanna take away from you.

00:46:40.348 --> 00:46:41.360
I can't talk about BiDil
in a couple minutes,

00:46:41.360 --> 00:46:43.520
but I'm gonna try to do it.

00:46:43.520 --> 00:46:48.020
So BiDil is a combination
of two generic drugs,

00:46:48.020 --> 00:46:51.280
Isosorbide dinitrate and
Hydralazine hydrochloride.

00:46:51.280 --> 00:46:54.020
These are two generic
drugs that have been used

00:46:54.020 --> 00:46:56.490
by cardiologists over the years,

00:46:56.490 --> 00:47:00.040
since the 1970s or before
to treat heart failure.

00:47:00.040 --> 00:47:01.140
And they've used these

00:47:02.060 --> 00:47:05.120
two generic drugs in
different combinations

00:47:05.120 --> 00:47:06.440
to treat people with heart failure.

00:47:06.440 --> 00:47:08.470
And when I say treat people, black,

00:47:08.470 --> 00:47:11.290
white, yellow, green, blue people,

00:47:11.290 --> 00:47:12.360
everybody, they use them

00:47:12.360 --> 00:47:14.600
and they'd use did in
different combinations

00:47:14.600 --> 00:47:16.660
to treat different people.

00:47:16.660 --> 00:47:20.230
In the 1970s, a researcher
at Minnesota is a clinician,

00:47:20.230 --> 00:47:23.530
J Kahn started using putting
these two drugs together

00:47:23.530 --> 00:47:24.923
in particular combinations.

00:47:25.931 --> 00:47:28.720
And over time he sold the rights

00:47:28.720 --> 00:47:30.280
to a company called NitroMed,

00:47:30.280 --> 00:47:32.430
got a patent on it and is a long story,

00:47:32.430 --> 00:47:34.660
they got three patents on it.

00:47:34.660 --> 00:47:39.660
They applied to the FDA in
the 80s to get it approved

00:47:39.840 --> 00:47:43.330
to treat heart failure in blacks

00:47:43.330 --> 00:47:45.880
because some study that
they had done had shown

00:47:45.880 --> 00:47:48.580
that it works better in black
people and other people.

00:47:48.580 --> 00:47:50.830
And they thought, okay, we need to get

00:47:50.830 --> 00:47:53.550
the FDA to approve this for
us to treat black people.

00:47:53.550 --> 00:47:55.820
Applied to the FDA, '96,
the FDA turned them down

00:47:55.820 --> 00:47:57.657
and said, "No, you need to go do a study

00:47:57.657 --> 00:48:00.750
that will show that it works better

00:48:00.750 --> 00:48:02.853
for black people than other people."

00:48:02.853 --> 00:48:04.320
So they designed the
study called the A-HeFT,

00:48:04.320 --> 00:48:06.780
African American Heart Failure Trial.

00:48:06.780 --> 00:48:10.313
And the trial started in 2001.

00:48:12.590 --> 00:48:15.660
And in 2003, they discontinued the trial.

00:48:15.660 --> 00:48:16.940
There was a placebo trial

00:48:16.940 --> 00:48:18.420
so there were half of the people

00:48:18.420 --> 00:48:21.130
at 1,050 people were in this study,

00:48:21.130 --> 00:48:24.250
half of them got placebo
and standard treatment

00:48:24.250 --> 00:48:26.160
and the standard treatment
was either ACE inhibitors

00:48:26.160 --> 00:48:27.680
or diuretics.

00:48:27.680 --> 00:48:30.090
So they got a placebo and standard

00:48:30.090 --> 00:48:33.963
and the other half got standard and BiDil.

00:48:34.975 --> 00:48:38.210
And so in 2003, they ended the trial

00:48:38.210 --> 00:48:41.490
because the group that was getting BiDil,

00:48:41.490 --> 00:48:44.040
they know when they compared the two,

00:48:44.040 --> 00:48:46.150
the group that was getting BiDil,

00:48:46.150 --> 00:48:49.310
had a reduction of death
from heart failure,

00:48:49.310 --> 00:48:52.480
43%, big percentage.

00:48:52.480 --> 00:48:54.195
So they had to end it

00:48:54.195 --> 00:48:55.280
because it would've been
unethical to continue.

00:48:55.280 --> 00:48:56.970
So the end of the trial, 2003.

00:48:56.970 --> 00:49:01.110
And at that time, sought
to get another patent,

00:49:01.110 --> 00:49:02.010
they did get another patent.

00:49:02.010 --> 00:49:03.850
They have had three patents,

00:49:03.850 --> 00:49:06.070
that the whole patent
story is a whole big story.

00:49:06.070 --> 00:49:08.700
Jonathan Kahn, who's
probably written the most

00:49:08.700 --> 00:49:12.890
on this has actually
written a book on BiDil.

00:49:12.890 --> 00:49:14.200
So you can look him up Jonathan Khan

00:49:14.200 --> 00:49:15.810
if you wanna learn more about that.

00:49:15.810 --> 00:49:17.180
But the interesting thing about BiDil,

00:49:17.180 --> 00:49:19.120
one of the interesting
things about BiDil is that,

00:49:19.120 --> 00:49:22.622
as I said before, is the first drug ever

00:49:22.622 --> 00:49:27.622
to be approved by the FDA for
a particular racial group.

00:49:28.740 --> 00:49:31.880
The Company, NitroMed once in 2005,

00:49:34.010 --> 00:49:36.720
it was approved by the FDA.

00:49:36.720 --> 00:49:39.080
in 2008, the company went out of business

00:49:40.150 --> 00:49:41.670
and a couple other companies bought it.

00:49:41.670 --> 00:49:44.850
It's now owned by a company
called Arbor Pharmaceuticals,

00:49:44.850 --> 00:49:47.310
which is where this came
from on their website.

00:49:47.310 --> 00:49:49.423
So they're still marketing the drug.

00:49:51.070 --> 00:49:52.870
There are a couple of people are...

00:49:52.870 --> 00:49:54.720
We don't have a ton of data.

00:49:54.720 --> 00:49:55.940
My team, and I have done

00:49:55.940 --> 00:50:00.440
some empirical studies on
physicians use of BiDil.

00:50:00.440 --> 00:50:02.540
This is a study we did with cardiologists,

00:50:03.480 --> 00:50:05.330
but there are really not many studies

00:50:06.242 --> 00:50:09.443
on BiDil and trying to understand
why it failed so early.

00:50:11.010 --> 00:50:14.670
The NAACP, the Congressional Black Caucus,

00:50:14.670 --> 00:50:16.350
the Association of Black Cardiologists

00:50:16.350 --> 00:50:20.350
all supported this at the time.

00:50:20.350 --> 00:50:22.940
Now, I talked to many of them
and many of them didn't know

00:50:22.940 --> 00:50:24.660
that this was what the company was going

00:50:24.660 --> 00:50:28.520
for to get it approved to
treat a specific racial group.

00:50:28.520 --> 00:50:30.660
I testified at the hearing for this,

00:50:30.660 --> 00:50:33.900
for the approval, my
colleagues and I at Howard,

00:50:33.900 --> 00:50:35.770
I was at Howard at the time.

00:50:35.770 --> 00:50:38.784
We were black folks
over on the side saying,

00:50:38.784 --> 00:50:40.460
"Do not approve this drug
just for black people."

00:50:40.460 --> 00:50:43.637
And then we have the
Congressional Black Caucus saying,

00:50:43.637 --> 00:50:45.100
"We've been discriminated against,

00:50:45.100 --> 00:50:47.576
give us our drug, it's our time.

00:50:47.576 --> 00:50:49.510
We gotta address health disparities."

00:50:49.510 --> 00:50:52.433
Very, very interesting hearing.

00:50:53.290 --> 00:50:55.370
The FDA ended up approving it.

00:50:55.370 --> 00:50:56.870
This was a study that we had done.

00:50:56.870 --> 00:50:58.040
There's so much I could see about this,

00:50:58.040 --> 00:51:00.912
but we don't have time. (laughs)

00:51:00.912 --> 00:51:02.850
These are some findings from a study

00:51:02.850 --> 00:51:05.800
we did some colleagues
and I did into 2019.

00:51:05.800 --> 00:51:08.220
We just published another
paper with some findings

00:51:08.220 --> 00:51:09.170
that were similar.

00:51:09.170 --> 00:51:12.640
But what we found is
that some cardiologists

00:51:12.640 --> 00:51:17.060
are still using BiDil,
but for most of them,

00:51:17.060 --> 00:51:19.640
they give it to anybody
for whom it will work.

00:51:19.640 --> 00:51:23.430
Is approved just for
self-identified blacks

00:51:23.430 --> 00:51:25.700
that's what the indication says.

00:51:25.700 --> 00:51:28.830
And so people are approving it off label

00:51:28.830 --> 00:51:30.960
are prescribing it off
label to other people

00:51:30.960 --> 00:51:32.580
for whom it works as we see there,

00:51:32.580 --> 00:51:34.990
not only given to black patients.

00:51:34.990 --> 00:51:35.860
And for many of them,

00:51:35.860 --> 00:51:38.540
the primary criterion was not race

00:51:38.540 --> 00:51:40.060
in terms of why they prescribed it.

00:51:40.060 --> 00:51:42.600
This was the indication
what else was going on

00:51:42.600 --> 00:51:45.093
with that person in terms of their health.

00:51:46.417 --> 00:51:47.250
And then there are some that believe

00:51:47.250 --> 00:51:49.876
that it was appropriate to do that,

00:51:49.876 --> 00:51:51.630
for the FDA to do that
and others said that.

00:51:51.630 --> 00:51:55.140
Bottom line is lots of
questions among physicians,

00:51:55.140 --> 00:51:57.990
even about BiDil and whether
they should have been done,

00:51:57.990 --> 00:52:00.740
but it's been done still the only case

00:52:00.740 --> 00:52:04.300
for which this has been
done, lots of issues there.

00:52:04.300 --> 00:52:06.795
And we may talk about some of them later

00:52:06.795 --> 00:52:08.520
about why the FDA saw it fit to do this

00:52:08.520 --> 00:52:10.510
for this drug and not for other drugs.

00:52:10.510 --> 00:52:12.350
For other studies where there were

00:52:12.350 --> 00:52:14.910
no black people over years.

00:52:14.910 --> 00:52:17.300
And so their thing was that this study was

00:52:17.300 --> 00:52:18.970
just done with black people

00:52:18.970 --> 00:52:20.950
and so it can only be
approved for black people.

00:52:20.950 --> 00:52:24.520
Well, studies long time ago
didn't include any black people

00:52:24.520 --> 00:52:27.650
and we get all those drugs
that all of us are using.

00:52:27.650 --> 00:52:29.020
And so why is it different?

00:52:29.020 --> 00:52:32.140
So lots and lots of issues there.

00:52:32.140 --> 00:52:35.930
I'm gonna, and this was
from that same study

00:52:35.930 --> 00:52:39.390
that our doctor said to us that
my white patients would walk

00:52:39.390 --> 00:52:42.648
in and say, "Give me that
black drug, is working for me."

00:52:42.648 --> 00:52:45.020
They just wanna know
what's working for them.

00:52:45.020 --> 00:52:47.430
So we put these labels on
these things and people,

00:52:47.430 --> 00:52:48.930
many people don't really care.

00:52:49.774 --> 00:52:51.230
So in terms of race based medicine,

00:52:51.230 --> 00:52:52.730
the paper that colleagues and I wrote

00:52:52.730 --> 00:52:54.330
about precision medicine, there's this

00:52:54.330 --> 00:52:56.410
all this talk about precision medicine.

00:52:56.410 --> 00:52:58.930
And we wrote this paper,
it wasn't a question.

00:52:58.930 --> 00:53:01.130
And we talked in that paper, we talked,

00:53:01.130 --> 00:53:02.680
you used some examples,

00:53:02.680 --> 00:53:04.740
but also talked about what it would take

00:53:04.740 --> 00:53:09.230
for us to move beyond race and
whether we can even do that.

00:53:09.230 --> 00:53:10.393
Then some colleagues,

00:53:12.300 --> 00:53:16.270
the same year wrote a piece on taking race

00:53:16.270 --> 00:53:18.460
out of human genetics studies.

00:53:18.460 --> 00:53:21.660
And that's been a big conversation about

00:53:21.660 --> 00:53:23.130
whether genetic and genomic studies

00:53:23.130 --> 00:53:26.663
should be even using the concept of race.

00:53:27.890 --> 00:53:30.140
And then we had this
study that was published

00:53:31.380 --> 00:53:33.310
a little later last year

00:53:33.310 --> 00:53:35.707
where some geneticists
and others are saying,

00:53:35.707 --> 00:53:39.460
"Hold up a minute, not so
quick with getting rid of race.

00:53:39.460 --> 00:53:42.082
There may be some utility to this."

00:53:42.082 --> 00:53:42.960
And these are geneticists

00:53:42.960 --> 00:53:45.610
and they gave some studies about how race,

00:53:45.610 --> 00:53:48.472
even more than genetic
ancestry in some cases

00:53:48.472 --> 00:53:50.930
was predictive of health outcomes.

00:53:50.930 --> 00:53:55.380
So there continues to be
the debate about this,

00:53:55.380 --> 00:53:57.640
and whether we use this
in genetic studies.

00:53:57.640 --> 00:54:00.250
I co-chair a National Academies
Committee that is looking

00:54:00.250 --> 00:54:03.503
at this very issues about
use of race and other

00:54:03.503 --> 00:54:06.040
such descriptors in genetic studies.

00:54:06.040 --> 00:54:07.950
I'm gonna move on quickly because

00:54:07.950 --> 00:54:11.663
I wanna do the Q&amp;A and we are past time.

00:54:13.330 --> 00:54:15.610
This slide and the next
couple slides are really

00:54:15.610 --> 00:54:18.550
just about where we are
in the conversations.

00:54:18.550 --> 00:54:21.030
So there's been a lot of
conversations recently in this

00:54:21.030 --> 00:54:25.120
since 2020 in the death
of George Floyd and all,

00:54:25.120 --> 00:54:27.414
a lot of things that have been happening

00:54:27.414 --> 00:54:30.385
about how we use race in
medicine and about algorithms.

00:54:30.385 --> 00:54:31.420
You've heard about the eGFR

00:54:31.420 --> 00:54:34.140
and how these algorithms
are used to describe

00:54:34.140 --> 00:54:37.640
to determine who gets what treatments.

00:54:37.640 --> 00:54:39.310
And that these algorithms are based

00:54:39.310 --> 00:54:40.350
on ideas about race.

00:54:40.350 --> 00:54:45.113
So eGFR, the estimated
glomerular filtration rate

00:54:45.113 --> 00:54:47.388
and this algorithm that has been used

00:54:47.388 --> 00:54:50.020
to give different
treatments to black patients

00:54:50.020 --> 00:54:51.624
from white patients,

00:54:51.624 --> 00:54:53.473
because of the prolonged
standing perception

00:54:53.473 --> 00:54:56.150
that black patients have more muscle mass

00:54:56.150 --> 00:54:57.020
than white patients

00:54:57.020 --> 00:54:59.210
and so they excrete more creatinine.

00:54:59.210 --> 00:55:01.330
So you look at a level of
creatinine and you have

00:55:01.330 --> 00:55:04.630
to adjust your algorithm
because black people

00:55:04.630 --> 00:55:06.120
have more muscle mass.

00:55:06.120 --> 00:55:09.760
So these ideas longstanding, but

00:55:09.760 --> 00:55:12.083
they influence what we do today.

00:55:13.239 --> 00:55:17.060
So wrapping up, I really am
wrapping up now. (chuckles)

00:55:17.060 --> 00:55:18.180
Where do we go from here?

00:55:18.180 --> 00:55:20.773
I presented a lot of
issues, a lot of problems.

00:55:22.258 --> 00:55:25.673
And I have here, what I call
the biomedical ecosystem

00:55:27.596 --> 00:55:29.290
is all of the entities,
and these may not be all,

00:55:29.290 --> 00:55:34.290
but some of the key ones
that have to be involved

00:55:34.680 --> 00:55:37.410
in any efforts to change

00:55:37.410 --> 00:55:39.720
what I've been talking
about this whole time

00:55:39.720 --> 00:55:43.363
to change how we use race in society,

00:55:43.363 --> 00:55:47.610
in health research, in clinical practice,

00:55:47.610 --> 00:55:49.830
it takes more than just the geneticists

00:55:49.830 --> 00:55:51.370
or the social scientists,

00:55:51.370 --> 00:55:54.470
bringing us back to our
conversation about how we think

00:55:54.470 --> 00:55:58.080
about knowledge and making knowledge and

00:55:59.009 --> 00:56:02.830
how we make ethical knowledge.

00:56:02.830 --> 00:56:05.390
It has to be informed by multiple voices

00:56:05.390 --> 00:56:08.520
for us to think through all of the issues.

00:56:08.520 --> 00:56:11.380
And one of the areas that I am gonna talk

00:56:11.380 --> 00:56:13.910
about just really quickly
is the area of training.

00:56:13.910 --> 00:56:15.360
There's a course that I teach

00:56:15.360 --> 00:56:17.653
at Duke called Race Genomics in Society,

00:56:18.593 --> 00:56:20.743
where I bring all of
these things together,

00:56:21.793 --> 00:56:23.180
bringing genetics and
I do a Pri on genetics

00:56:23.180 --> 00:56:26.160
and talk about how genetics is influences

00:56:26.160 --> 00:56:30.080
and how people think about
athletics and intelligence

00:56:30.080 --> 00:56:32.140
and skin color and help them understand

00:56:32.140 --> 00:56:35.737
how these things come to be
and what genetics says or not

00:56:35.737 --> 00:56:39.523
and how society says about these things.

00:56:39.523 --> 00:56:40.560
And then talking about help.

00:56:40.560 --> 00:56:43.150
And this fall, I'm always
doing different things

00:56:43.150 --> 00:56:43.983
with this class.

00:56:43.983 --> 00:56:46.460
And this fall, last fall,

00:56:46.460 --> 00:56:51.460
I had the experience of a lifetime

00:56:51.520 --> 00:56:52.970
where I asked the students and I've kept

00:56:52.970 --> 00:56:54.483
that course at 100 students.

00:56:55.409 --> 00:56:56.755
So this semester, this fall,

00:56:56.755 --> 00:57:00.350
we had 96 students who 98
started in 96 at the class.

00:57:00.350 --> 00:57:01.460
And one of their assignments

00:57:01.460 --> 00:57:05.710
was to create a piece of
art at the end of the class

00:57:05.710 --> 00:57:07.033
for throughout the class,

00:57:07.892 --> 00:57:09.453
but a piece of art that represented

00:57:09.453 --> 00:57:11.310
what they took away

00:57:11.310 --> 00:57:15.030
and it had to be a piece of
art that brought together

00:57:15.030 --> 00:57:19.320
the genetics and the social
and all of the factors.

00:57:19.320 --> 00:57:23.090
And they could focus on
one module, say skin color,

00:57:23.090 --> 00:57:25.970
or they could do a
combination, ancestry testing,

00:57:25.970 --> 00:57:27.720
skin color, intelligence, whatever.

00:57:29.014 --> 00:57:31.540
And the students created, they
had an eight and a half 11,

00:57:31.540 --> 00:57:34.520
I worked with people in my arts department

00:57:34.520 --> 00:57:36.660
and it was it's four discussion sections

00:57:36.660 --> 00:57:38.070
and they created these art

00:57:38.070 --> 00:57:40.510
and these are the four panels.

00:57:40.510 --> 00:57:42.842
And I can't tell,

00:57:42.842 --> 00:57:45.430
and each of them had to
write a 150 word statement

00:57:45.430 --> 00:57:48.360
that described their piece.

00:57:48.360 --> 00:57:51.540
It is now on display in
our Rubinstein Art Center,

00:57:51.540 --> 00:57:53.973
which is our big center
Art Center at Duke.

00:57:54.940 --> 00:57:56.760
And I can't tell you what it means for me

00:57:56.760 --> 00:57:59.100
to see what they wrote,

00:57:59.100 --> 00:58:01.780
not because it says anything about me,

00:58:01.780 --> 00:58:05.140
but because it tells me about the future,

00:58:05.140 --> 00:58:09.230
it gives me hope for the future
and how these students took

00:58:09.230 --> 00:58:13.000
this information in and how
it informs how they think

00:58:13.000 --> 00:58:17.140
about race and racism
and what they will do

00:58:17.140 --> 00:58:17.973
in the future.

00:58:17.973 --> 00:58:20.920
So I could talk forever about that.

00:58:20.920 --> 00:58:22.960
I sat and I read all of
these and I'm thinking,

00:58:22.960 --> 00:58:25.560
did they really get this from this?

00:58:25.560 --> 00:58:27.010
Fascinating, fascinating.

00:58:27.010 --> 00:58:29.090
And then some of them
would say, "I'm no artist."

00:58:29.090 --> 00:58:31.325
When you see what they created,

00:58:31.325 --> 00:58:32.920
and I said, we are all
artists, it was just great.

00:58:32.920 --> 00:58:37.110
I'm gonna end with a slide
by a colleague of mine,

00:58:37.110 --> 00:58:38.940
building on how we teach,

00:58:38.940 --> 00:58:41.660
how we teach about race and genetics

00:58:41.660 --> 00:58:45.080
because for me, how we
communicate the information,

00:58:45.080 --> 00:58:47.500
and I say we, I'm talking about faculty

00:58:47.500 --> 00:58:50.250
in the academy and others who teach

00:58:50.250 --> 00:58:53.692
and how we help people
understand social race

00:58:53.692 --> 00:58:54.910
and biological race,

00:58:54.910 --> 00:58:56.830
and the difference between the two

00:58:56.830 --> 00:58:59.123
and what happens when we conflate the two,

00:59:00.020 --> 00:59:02.680
and teaching people so they understand.

00:59:02.680 --> 00:59:07.070
And so teaching about this has
been one of my life mission

00:59:07.070 --> 00:59:11.236
and a colleague of mine,
Brian Donovan, who works

00:59:11.236 --> 00:59:16.236
in Colorado at the Biological
Sciences Curriculum Study,

00:59:18.110 --> 00:59:20.830
he's a research scientist there has been,

00:59:20.830 --> 00:59:24.313
he works, they work
primarily with K through 12.

00:59:25.504 --> 00:59:28.283
And when I finish my Race
Genomics and Society Class,

00:59:28.283 --> 00:59:31.090
we always do a so what
at the end of the class,

00:59:31.090 --> 00:59:33.190
and invariably students say

00:59:33.190 --> 00:59:35.390
this information needs
to be taught earlier,

00:59:36.572 --> 00:59:39.147
I didn't learn this until I got here.

00:59:39.147 --> 00:59:40.810
And Brian and his team are,

00:59:40.810 --> 00:59:42.626
they have been doing studies

00:59:42.626 --> 00:59:44.933
and have done studies that
show that how we teach.

00:59:45.792 --> 00:59:48.172
And I talked about Mendelian
disorders and how we teach

00:59:48.172 --> 00:59:50.377
about Mendelian disorders and

00:59:50.377 --> 00:59:52.610
how we teach about the
presence and absence of genes

00:59:52.610 --> 00:59:54.720
and how that leads to an outcome

00:59:54.720 --> 00:59:56.620
and needing to teach about complexity.

00:59:57.530 --> 01:00:01.460
How we teach students at
all levels influences.

01:00:01.460 --> 01:00:03.970
And they've done randomized
trials that show that students

01:00:03.970 --> 01:00:06.700
who learn about complexity
have different ideas

01:00:06.700 --> 01:00:08.650
about the relationships between people,

01:00:09.590 --> 01:00:11.407
about what people are capable of

01:00:11.407 --> 01:00:14.923
and what genetic differences
mean or don't mean.

01:00:16.633 --> 01:00:18.430
And he and I collaborating on some,

01:00:18.430 --> 01:00:20.770
getting ready to
collaborate on some things.

01:00:20.770 --> 01:00:23.599
So thinking of the ethics
of knowledge making

01:00:23.599 --> 01:00:25.120
toward a more humane genetics,

01:00:25.120 --> 01:00:26.980
how we think about justice,

01:00:26.980 --> 01:00:30.370
how we think about beneficence
and non maleficence

01:00:30.370 --> 01:00:34.010
in our teaching about race and genetics

01:00:34.010 --> 01:00:36.060
and how those things come together,

01:00:36.060 --> 01:00:40.687
leading us to a more humane
genetics and a more humane way

01:00:40.687 --> 01:00:44.200
of thinking about the
relationships between these things,

01:00:44.200 --> 01:00:48.210
but a more humane way of
living with one another.

01:00:48.210 --> 01:00:50.781
I'm gonna stop there. Thank you.

01:00:50.781 --> 01:00:53.948
(audience applauding)

