because it's one that is a barrier,

01:07:34.100 --> 01:07:38.990
I think to the the country
understanding in this complexity.

01:07:38.990 --> 01:07:41.040
- That's a really good question, Brandon.

01:07:42.890 --> 01:07:45.277
I have talked with my
colleagues in the CBC

01:07:46.512 --> 01:07:49.280
and at the NAACP about their

01:07:53.720 --> 01:07:56.760
myopic way of looking at this.

01:07:56.760 --> 01:08:00.410
Of course, they have an agenda that

01:08:00.410 --> 01:08:02.123
in some ways different from mine.

01:08:03.517 --> 01:08:07.630
And their agenda is to
ensure that the policies

01:08:07.630 --> 01:08:11.543
that are put in place in their
minds benefit black people.

01:08:12.720 --> 01:08:14.090
When I talk to them,

01:08:14.090 --> 01:08:17.496
they understand the
scientific racism argument

01:08:17.496 --> 01:08:18.823
that we were trying to make,

01:08:18.823 --> 01:08:20.660
that approving this drug just

01:08:20.660 --> 01:08:23.160
for black people is sending the message

01:08:23.160 --> 01:08:26.750
that many already believe
that black people are

01:08:26.750 --> 01:08:29.763
different biologically
and need their own drug.

01:08:31.860 --> 01:08:36.860
And so I had them think about
that and for them it's like,

01:08:37.200 --> 01:08:39.350
we know that, but

01:08:41.060 --> 01:08:43.920
we still need some attention.

01:08:43.920 --> 01:08:47.153
- Oh, interesting.

01:08:47.153 --> 01:08:48.683
- It's a tough one,

01:08:49.820 --> 01:08:53.710
but it requires those
kinds of conversations

01:08:53.710 --> 01:08:55.633
to get us across the divides.

01:08:57.360 --> 01:09:02.320
When I talk to them about their
roles in the community and

01:09:02.320 --> 01:09:05.449
how they need to think about the roles

01:09:05.449 --> 01:09:07.950
that they play in reifying these notions

01:09:07.950 --> 01:09:10.977
and how the people within
the black community,

01:09:10.977 --> 01:09:15.060
"black community", I
use that term in quotes,

01:09:15.060 --> 01:09:18.070
look up to them to
protect their interests.

01:09:18.070 --> 01:09:21.230
And if their interests, at least
in my view are not informed

01:09:23.961 --> 01:09:27.303
are not looking at the big picture.

01:09:28.450 --> 01:09:29.860
In their minds, they're
looking at the big picture,

01:09:29.860 --> 01:09:31.719
which is social justice.

01:09:31.719 --> 01:09:33.810
I'm like, I'm looking at
social justice too, but

01:09:33.810 --> 01:09:34.710
we need to figure it out.

01:09:34.710 --> 01:09:37.060
The problem, the thing
is that if it's approved

01:09:37.911 --> 01:09:41.350
for everyone, black people
will get it too, hopefully.

01:09:41.350 --> 01:09:42.183
- You're right.

01:09:42.183 --> 01:09:43.412
That's a different problem.

01:09:43.412 --> 01:09:44.510
(both laughing)

01:09:44.510 --> 01:09:45.560
- They're get it too.

01:09:47.740 --> 01:09:51.260
when I think about justice
and social justice,

01:09:51.260 --> 01:09:53.430
I think about justice for everybody.

01:09:53.430 --> 01:09:57.440
So I think about the Hispanic
people who won't get it

01:09:57.440 --> 01:09:59.640
because it's just
approved for black people.

01:10:00.603 --> 01:10:01.673
But the other issue is that,

01:10:02.540 --> 01:10:05.000
it's not gonna work for every black person

01:10:05.000 --> 01:10:06.600
as we saw in the trial.

01:10:06.600 --> 01:10:10.110
So what about those people who are black,

01:10:10.110 --> 01:10:15.110
who won't respond well to
BiDil, will they get a drug?

01:10:16.300 --> 01:10:18.083
So once we go down this path,

01:10:19.150 --> 01:10:23.950
it may look like it's serving a purpose

01:10:23.950 --> 01:10:26.890
and doing what they think it's gonna do,

01:10:26.890 --> 01:10:31.890
but the downsides to not just
people who identify as black

01:10:32.150 --> 01:10:34.300
and they're thinking that, okay,

01:10:34.300 --> 01:10:35.600
we've given them their drug,

01:10:35.600 --> 01:10:38.020
we don't have to try to find another one

01:10:38.020 --> 01:10:39.810
that might work for them.

01:10:39.810 --> 01:10:42.180
Now, how BiDil has played out in terms

01:10:42.180 --> 01:10:46.840
of Arbor Pharmaceuticals is
the company's marketing it,

01:10:46.840 --> 01:10:48.490
doctors are still prescribing it,

01:10:49.340 --> 01:10:51.140
many black people are not taking it.

01:10:52.480 --> 01:10:56.470
I remember in 2005, when
it was approved in 2006,

01:10:56.470 --> 01:10:58.670
and I would talk to
people, I was talking to

01:10:59.549 --> 01:11:02.880
this man who he was an
uncle of a friend of mine,

01:11:02.880 --> 01:11:04.380
and she said, "You need to talk

01:11:04.380 --> 01:11:06.250
to my uncle 'cause he won't take BiDil

01:11:07.183 --> 01:11:09.550
and he has heart failure."

01:11:09.550 --> 01:11:11.547
And I talked to him, I said,

01:11:11.547 --> 01:11:12.970
"So why won't you take the drugs?"

01:11:12.970 --> 01:11:15.883
He's like, "They developed
that just for us?

01:11:16.820 --> 01:11:18.737
Oh no, I'm not gonna take it."

01:11:19.910 --> 01:11:21.050
And so we think we're...

01:11:21.050 --> 01:11:22.770
And for many people that's probably

01:11:22.770 --> 01:11:25.300
why the drug didn't survive.

01:11:25.300 --> 01:11:28.160
And also the drug...

01:11:28.160 --> 01:11:28.993
There's so many stories.

01:11:28.993 --> 01:11:31.359
Many doctors were not
prescribing it for their patients

01:11:31.359 --> 01:11:34.470
'cause it was too expensive
for their black patients.

01:11:34.470 --> 01:11:37.550
And so there's so many
issues that surround...

01:11:37.550 --> 01:11:40.540
It is the first drug that's
done that this has happened to.

01:11:40.540 --> 01:11:42.383
So it's the only example we have.

01:11:43.300 --> 01:11:47.030
And we haven't done enough
research to really understand all

01:11:47.030 --> 01:11:51.510
the facets of what is
happening with BiDil.

01:11:51.510 --> 01:11:53.600
And so I think it requires conversation.

01:11:53.600 --> 01:11:55.300
I've had conversations with my colleagues

01:11:55.300 --> 01:11:59.660
at the CBC and at the NAACP about this.

01:11:59.660 --> 01:12:02.650
And over time, they got
upset because the drug,

01:12:02.650 --> 01:12:04.710
the price of the drug was so high.

01:12:04.710 --> 01:12:09.710
The drug had the highest markup
of any FDA approved, 700%

01:12:10.680 --> 01:12:14.080
after it was approved by the FDA, 700%.

01:12:14.080 --> 01:12:14.913
And going into this,

01:12:14.913 --> 01:12:17.300
people were worried and
were saying to NitroMed,

01:12:19.960 --> 01:12:22.410
black people, if you say
this drug for black people

01:12:22.410 --> 01:12:25.620
and many people who not able to afford it,

01:12:25.620 --> 01:12:27.898
you need to make it
affordable and say, oh,

01:12:27.898 --> 01:12:29.790
it's gonna be afforded,
but what it was not.

01:12:29.790 --> 01:12:33.630
And so the cost became an an issue.

01:12:33.630 --> 01:12:34.600
And so I think

01:12:36.707 --> 01:12:37.657
when we think about

01:12:40.820 --> 01:12:42.813
how these things play out,

01:12:43.860 --> 01:12:45.380
we have to have a broad view

01:12:47.997 --> 01:12:48.830
of things

01:12:50.867 --> 01:12:53.927
and prioritize, figure out
what's really important.

01:12:56.490 --> 01:12:58.840
- Really, really important

01:12:58.840 --> 01:13:00.180
'cause every time there's this big,

01:13:00.180 --> 01:13:01.910
there's a lot of big public hiccups

01:13:01.910 --> 01:13:03.880
around talking about race,
David Reich, talk about,

01:13:03.880 --> 01:13:05.253
they always use disease,

01:13:06.171 --> 01:13:08.167
these types of things about how,

01:13:08.167 --> 01:13:09.170
and I think the way you've been able

01:13:10.286 --> 01:13:12.338
to disentangle that is really profound.

01:13:12.338 --> 01:13:13.171
I'll ask one last question

01:13:13.171 --> 01:13:15.310
and then I'll take
questions from the audience

01:13:16.320 --> 01:13:18.113
while we have a few minutes.

01:13:19.040 --> 01:13:20.729
just I want to get in,

01:13:20.729 --> 01:13:22.344
just go to sickle cell anemia.

01:13:22.344 --> 01:13:24.760
I think your work there was
my introduction to your work.

01:13:24.760 --> 01:13:26.240
And I think what's beautiful about it

01:13:26.240 --> 01:13:27.870
and certainly Professor Near,

01:13:27.870 --> 01:13:32.870
I think we teach that as the
canonical Mendelian disease.

01:13:33.220 --> 01:13:35.540
And I think we have these
other models of genetics where

01:13:35.540 --> 01:13:37.293
everything else is more complex,

01:13:38.200 --> 01:13:40.660
but sickle cell is
very, very, very simple.

01:13:40.660 --> 01:13:43.610
And I think what your work
has done has blown that open

01:13:43.610 --> 01:13:47.400
and articulated that, even that
one, now I think to myself,

01:13:47.400 --> 01:13:48.980
wow, is Mendelian disease,

01:13:48.980 --> 01:13:51.740
even a proper term for things.

01:13:51.740 --> 01:13:54.280
Maybe we needed entire new lexicon

01:13:54.280 --> 01:13:56.200
to describe genetics as a whole.

01:13:56.200 --> 01:13:58.350
You're succeeding in making all

01:13:58.350 --> 01:13:59.591
of our jobs more difficult.

01:13:59.591 --> 01:14:02.117
(Royal laughs)

01:14:02.117 --> 01:14:04.820
I want you to lean into that
and are there other diseases

01:14:04.820 --> 01:14:05.900
you talked about that this is not the

01:14:05.900 --> 01:14:07.350
only racialized disease,

01:14:07.350 --> 01:14:08.380
as you say, maybe the most,

01:14:08.380 --> 01:14:10.954
but kind of lean into that a little bit

01:14:10.954 --> 01:14:11.787
and what are some other
kind of thoughts you have

01:14:11.787 --> 01:14:13.090
in that direction or other diseases

01:14:13.090 --> 01:14:15.240
of that kind or wherever?

01:14:15.240 --> 01:14:17.133
- Okay, so good question.

01:14:18.910 --> 01:14:21.921
So there's cystic fibrosis,
which is also racialized,

01:14:21.921 --> 01:14:23.827
but not in the same way.

01:14:23.827 --> 01:14:25.430
But very similarly black people

01:14:25.430 --> 01:14:27.110
who have cystic fibrosis,

01:14:27.110 --> 01:14:29.450
they're misdiagnosed
just like white people

01:14:29.450 --> 01:14:31.520
who have sickle cell
disease are misdiagnosed,

01:14:31.520 --> 01:14:34.513
and misdiagnose, as we know,
can be a major problem.

01:14:35.480 --> 01:14:38.950
But cystic fibrosis has the
same kind of a complexity,

01:14:38.950 --> 01:14:41.370
smoke exposure and da, da, da.

01:14:41.370 --> 01:14:42.950
I just published a paper,

01:14:42.950 --> 01:14:45.480
well, it was published last
year as well with colleagues

01:14:45.480 --> 01:14:48.280
at the National Institute of
Environmental Health Sciences,

01:14:48.280 --> 01:14:50.730
looking at sickle cell
disease, cystic fibrosis,

01:14:50.730 --> 01:14:53.040
Huntington disease and Parkinson's disease

01:14:53.040 --> 01:14:54.720
as four monogenic diseases.

01:14:54.720 --> 01:14:57.670
Parkinson's is a little
more variable in terms

01:14:57.670 --> 01:15:01.860
of inheritance and looking
at environmental factors.

01:15:01.860 --> 01:15:04.390
So the paper was on
environmental influences

01:15:04.390 --> 01:15:06.343
on Mendelian diseases.

01:15:07.535 --> 01:15:08.536
And for us to start thinking

01:15:08.536 --> 01:15:10.440
about Mendelian diseases different.

01:15:10.440 --> 01:15:11.273
But your point

01:15:11.273 --> 01:15:15.520
about whether Mendelian
is even a way of thinking

01:15:15.520 --> 01:15:17.320
about it, guess we could say Mendelian,

01:15:17.320 --> 01:15:20.904
but probably not monogenic. (chuckles)

01:15:20.904 --> 01:15:24.734
Mendelian still helps
us predict inheritance

01:15:24.734 --> 01:15:26.250
and that's really

01:15:27.624 --> 01:15:30.360
the pattern, is the
patterns of inheritance.

01:15:30.360 --> 01:15:33.390
So the patterns are still there
in terms of how you get it

01:15:33.390 --> 01:15:36.540
'cause you can't get it,
these diseases without...

01:15:36.540 --> 01:15:38.710
Parkinson's, again is a little different,

01:15:38.710 --> 01:15:40.960
but Huntington and sickle cell and CF

01:15:42.141 --> 01:15:45.720
you at least right now, as far as we know,

01:15:45.720 --> 01:15:48.170
you can only get it by
inheriting those variants.

01:15:50.631 --> 01:15:53.933
So how we think about disease and health,

01:15:55.886 --> 01:15:58.770
somebody who has two S hemoglobins,

01:16:00.340 --> 01:16:03.290
but have no symptoms have no complication,

01:16:03.290 --> 01:16:05.050
they're pretty much fine.

01:16:05.050 --> 01:16:07.147
Do we say they have sickle cell disease?

01:16:07.147 --> 01:16:08.370
Do we say they have a disease?

01:16:08.370 --> 01:16:09.978
Well, we may or may not.

01:16:09.978 --> 01:16:13.850
And how we think about health and disease,

01:16:13.850 --> 01:16:16.070
all of those questions are part of

01:16:16.070 --> 01:16:19.000
this issue of complexity and how

01:16:19.000 --> 01:16:20.950
we understand complexity.

01:16:20.950 --> 01:16:23.050
And the more we look at...

01:16:23.050 --> 01:16:25.700
So my thing has always been
all diseases are complex.

01:16:26.560 --> 01:16:27.990
All diseases are complex.

01:16:27.990 --> 01:16:29.430
They don't just happen.

01:16:29.430 --> 01:16:32.220
There are multifactorial, all of them.

01:16:32.220 --> 01:16:34.750
Some may be a little
more complex than others

01:16:34.750 --> 01:16:36.940
or some, we may be able to predict

01:16:38.020 --> 01:16:41.910
their inheritance a little bit
better than other diseases.

01:16:41.910 --> 01:16:44.410
We know most breast cancers are sporadic,

01:16:44.410 --> 01:16:48.893
but about 10% and prostate
cancer are hereditary.

01:16:51.843 --> 01:16:53.460
And so thinking about diseases,

01:16:53.460 --> 01:16:56.983
all of them as being complex
and the levels of complexity.

01:16:59.120 --> 01:17:02.180
For the Mendelian disorders
or monogenic disorders,

01:17:02.180 --> 01:17:06.243
the environmental and social
factors may play a lesser,

01:17:08.590 --> 01:17:11.440
not prominent a role in their etiology,

01:17:11.440 --> 01:17:15.490
but in the pathophysiology,
they play a major role.

01:17:15.490 --> 01:17:18.311
And the more we understand those things,

01:17:18.311 --> 01:17:19.144
the better we are able to...

01:17:19.144 --> 01:17:20.180
Sickle cell disease is also the thing

01:17:20.180 --> 01:17:22.359
at the top of the list for gene editing.

01:17:22.359 --> 01:17:23.192
- That's right. That's right.

01:17:23.192 --> 01:17:24.760
- And my thing is that even when we think

01:17:24.760 --> 01:17:28.318
about who's gonna respond
well to curative therapies,

01:17:28.318 --> 01:17:30.550
we need to think about
these other factors.

01:17:30.550 --> 01:17:33.260
It's not just plugging in a gene here

01:17:33.260 --> 01:17:34.860
and everything is gonna be fine.

01:17:37.230 --> 01:17:40.010
- Yeah, no, so much. Great.

01:17:40.010 --> 01:17:41.090
Now thank you for that.

01:17:41.090 --> 01:17:42.240
We have a few minutes left,

01:17:42.240 --> 01:17:44.920
so we'll take a question
or two, if we can,

01:17:44.920 --> 01:17:47.421
from the audience, I think, no, no, no.

01:17:47.421 --> 01:17:48.662
We have a question.

01:17:48.662 --> 01:17:51.079
(indistinct)

01:18:22.950 --> 01:18:24.640
- Very good question.

01:18:24.640 --> 01:18:28.300
What I would say, and I did
not talk about racism as much,

01:18:28.300 --> 01:18:32.180
but I always say there's
no race without racism.

01:18:32.180 --> 01:18:34.023
Racism created race.

01:18:34.920 --> 01:18:38.400
And racism is what brings this hierarchy

01:18:38.400 --> 01:18:42.620
and these ideas about
inferiority and superiority

01:18:42.620 --> 01:18:45.510
that drove the creation of race.

01:18:45.510 --> 01:18:48.600
So we had these ideas about
who was most beautiful.

01:18:48.600 --> 01:18:51.790
We, Blumenbach whomever
about who was most beautiful

01:18:51.790 --> 01:18:55.730
and then we created something
to justify those ideas.

01:18:55.730 --> 01:18:58.210
So racism and race to me are intertwined.

01:18:58.210 --> 01:19:00.583
They're not separate,
they're not separable.

01:19:01.574 --> 01:19:05.420
And you're right, I think in many cases,

01:19:05.420 --> 01:19:09.090
we talk about race as a
category without talking

01:19:09.090 --> 01:19:11.500
about the racism that goes along with it.

01:19:11.500 --> 01:19:13.960
And I guess I did some of that
today talking about race and

01:19:13.960 --> 01:19:16.550
not so much the racism, but for me,

01:19:16.550 --> 01:19:20.450
they're all part of when I
talk about sickle cell disease

01:19:20.450 --> 01:19:22.320
and how people are treated in the clinic,

01:19:22.320 --> 01:19:23.870
it's because they're racialized

01:19:25.335 --> 01:19:26.864
that they're treated in a particular way.

01:19:26.864 --> 01:19:29.850
And so they're one and the same to me.

01:19:29.850 --> 01:19:31.150
And so

01:19:31.150 --> 01:19:34.383
if we say race is a
social construct, it's so,

01:19:36.048 --> 01:19:37.820
I hesitate to use, I know,

01:19:37.820 --> 01:19:40.980
I rarely say that, race
is a social construct,

01:19:40.980 --> 01:19:43.330
as opposed to saying it's
socially constructed.

01:19:44.320 --> 01:19:45.263
We made it up.

01:19:47.640 --> 01:19:52.100
And using it as a verb
as opposed to a noun,

01:19:52.100 --> 01:19:55.570
puts the owners on who
created it on the creation.

01:19:55.570 --> 01:19:57.260
So I agree with you

01:19:57.260 --> 01:20:00.207
that the racism is what
we need to address.

01:20:00.207 --> 01:20:04.340
And the racism is what
to address the racism

01:20:04.340 --> 01:20:07.723
in my view will take
us coming together and

01:20:09.220 --> 01:20:12.030
getting beyond the separation

01:20:12.030 --> 01:20:14.150
that has caused these divides

01:20:15.138 --> 01:20:16.660
and helping us, as I say very often,

01:20:16.660 --> 01:20:19.730
to see ourselves in the face of the other

01:20:19.730 --> 01:20:21.580
and to interact more with each other.

01:20:25.993 --> 01:20:30.508
- Any other questions from the audience?

01:20:30.508 --> 01:20:33.083
- I talk so much, you have
no questions. Sorry about.

01:20:33.083 --> 01:20:34.826
- No, I think there's one.

01:20:34.826 --> 01:20:35.930
We see if we can say.

01:20:35.930 --> 01:20:37.532
- [Participant 3] Well, there are a couple

01:20:37.532 --> 01:20:42.532
of short related questions (indistinct)

01:22:30.702 --> 01:22:31.535
- Okay.

01:22:31.535 --> 01:22:32.700
So I think that

01:22:34.530 --> 01:22:38.610
the conversations about race and racism

01:22:38.610 --> 01:22:42.163
and the conversations
about gender and sex,

01:22:43.070 --> 01:22:46.890
there are some similarities in
those conversations in terms

01:22:48.792 --> 01:22:53.792
of utilizing biology as a
marker of difference now,

01:22:56.350 --> 01:23:00.000
but they're different in
terms of how we use that.

01:23:00.000 --> 01:23:05.000
I know researchers who
study homosexuality,

01:23:05.318 --> 01:23:06.151
for example, and

01:23:07.192 --> 01:23:11.480
who identify as gay and
their work is intended

01:23:11.480 --> 01:23:14.793
to show that this is a biological trait.

01:23:15.800 --> 01:23:16.763
Race is different.

01:23:18.050 --> 01:23:20.340
For race, we're trying to show that

01:23:20.340 --> 01:23:22.970
what we're calling race and racism

01:23:22.970 --> 01:23:25.130
is socially constructed so biology

01:23:26.029 --> 01:23:28.550
is not the driver of those differences.

01:23:28.550 --> 01:23:31.180
So there are differences
in those conversations

01:23:31.180 --> 01:23:35.800
and in the underlying science
of those conversations.

01:23:35.800 --> 01:23:39.123
There's also a difference
with race in my view.

01:23:40.430 --> 01:23:42.210
I don't apologize for this.

01:23:42.210 --> 01:23:46.510
I think there's something
different about race from gender,

01:23:46.510 --> 01:23:50.370
from sexuality and any of
those other identities.

01:23:50.370 --> 01:23:53.033
I know about intersectionality as well,

01:23:54.324 --> 01:23:56.330
but if we do not focus,

01:23:56.330 --> 01:23:58.830
if we focus on race and
we can address race,

01:23:58.830 --> 01:24:01.920
then in my view, we can
address everything else.

01:24:01.920 --> 01:24:06.740
If we don't single race
out and address it,

01:24:06.740 --> 01:24:08.760
or at least try to address it,

01:24:08.760 --> 01:24:13.110
it constantly gets subsumed
on all these other things and

01:24:13.110 --> 01:24:16.330
never really gets the attention it needs.

01:24:16.330 --> 01:24:18.610
And race this country,

01:24:18.610 --> 01:24:22.893
and parts of the world
too is built on that idea.

01:24:23.920 --> 01:24:27.090
It is built on that separation.

01:24:27.090 --> 01:24:30.680
And so there is an urgent
need to address race

01:24:30.680 --> 01:24:33.830
like no other identity in my view.

01:24:33.830 --> 01:24:35.770
And so that's my perspective

01:24:36.924 --> 01:24:40.840
on race and racism in
light of these other isms

01:24:40.840 --> 01:24:42.900
that we are trying to address.

01:24:45.080 --> 01:24:46.520
The issue of...

01:24:50.071 --> 01:24:51.473
I'm blanking on the other question now.

01:24:52.633 --> 01:24:55.050
(indistinct)

01:25:01.260 --> 01:25:02.093
All right.

01:25:02.093 --> 01:25:05.180
I will say very quickly (chuckles)

01:25:06.640 --> 01:25:08.810
that I can't say a whole lot about that

01:25:08.810 --> 01:25:10.690
because I'm on this National
Academies Committee,

01:25:10.690 --> 01:25:12.040
that's looking at that,

01:25:12.040 --> 01:25:12.940
and we can't say much

01:25:12.940 --> 01:25:15.580
of people here have been
on National Academies here.

01:25:16.422 --> 01:25:19.172
(Brandon laughs)

01:25:21.060 --> 01:25:23.718
You're vowed to secrecy.

01:25:23.718 --> 01:25:25.180
So we're beginning that work.

01:25:25.180 --> 01:25:27.741
But I can say that,

01:25:27.741 --> 01:25:29.842
and the committee is comprised
of social scientists,

01:25:29.842 --> 01:25:32.463
epidemiologists, historians,
geneticists, and others.

01:25:33.677 --> 01:25:35.800
I think in the next 10 years, we will see,

01:25:35.800 --> 01:25:40.620
at least I hope we will see a shift in

01:25:40.620 --> 01:25:43.970
how genetic studies are done

01:25:45.250 --> 01:25:47.263
in light of what we know about race.

01:25:48.392 --> 01:25:51.580
And I think if we see a
shift in genetic studies

01:25:51.580 --> 01:25:53.440
that will inform what happens

01:25:53.440 --> 01:25:55.563
in biomedical research at large.

01:25:57.106 --> 01:25:58.730
I think genetics and the
role that genetics plays

01:25:58.730 --> 01:26:00.230
in the creation of race

01:26:00.230 --> 01:26:04.040
and the maintenance of this notion race.

01:26:04.040 --> 01:26:06.764
I think if genetics and geneticists

01:26:06.764 --> 01:26:11.190
and genomicists change
the way they do research,

01:26:11.190 --> 01:26:13.990
and when I say change, I'm
talking about everything from

01:26:14.940 --> 01:26:16.430
whether we use race or not,

01:26:16.430 --> 01:26:19.620
but how we think about the role of genes

01:26:19.620 --> 01:26:22.770
in outcomes, health, outcomes, traits,

01:26:22.770 --> 01:26:24.210
whatever the outcomes are,

01:26:24.210 --> 01:26:27.920
how geneticists think about
and design their studies,

01:26:27.920 --> 01:26:31.826
I think will inform what
other disciplines do.

01:26:31.826 --> 01:26:34.610
What I think a change is on the horizon.

01:26:34.610 --> 01:26:37.760
I hope it is. (laughs)

01:26:37.760 --> 01:26:40.628
- Great. Well, join me in congratulating.

01:26:40.628 --> 01:26:41.501
Thank you.

01:26:41.501 --> 01:26:44.668
(audience applauding)

01:26:45.616 --> 01:26:48.033
(indistinct)

01:26:52.402 --> 01:26:54.112
(table bangs)

01:26:54.112 --> 01:26:57.291
I have so much talk to you about.

01:26:57.291 --> 01:26:58.291
Please be my friend.

